scriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific schematic figure, panel labeled **“b.”**, titled **“Model Architecture and Training Procedure.”**\n\nIt depicts a computational biology pipeline combining **H&E imaging** and **spatial sequencing** data for **spatial transcriptomics prediction**.\n\nVisible elements include:\n\n- **Input biological data sources**\n  - H&E imaging, shown with a microscope icon and tissue image tiles.\n  - Spatial sequencing, shown with an instrument icon and a grid-like spatial transcriptomics matrix.\n\n- **Feature extraction / model components**\n  - An **Image FM** branch processes histology image patches.\n  - An **ST FM** branch processes spatial transcriptomics features.\n  - A diameter-based feature extraction inset shows circular regions at approximate scales labeled **2 µm**, **55 µm**, and **150 µm**.\n  - Extracted image and ST feature vectors are fed into a **Contrastive Learning** module.\n\n- **Contrastive learning**\n  - A similarity matrix is shown, with diagonal matching entries highlighted.\n  - Feature vectors from image and spatial transcriptomics modalities are aligned.\n  - The output connects to a convolutional block labeled **“8 Neighbors”**, followed by **“Conv & Concat.”**\n\n- **Fusion and prediction**\n  - A large yellow module labeled **FiLM** receives concatenated convolutional features and diameter/context features.\n  - The FiLM-modulated representation is passed to **ST Prediction**, producing a predicted spatial transcriptomics grid.\n\n- **Legend / training status**\n  - Gray arrows indicate **Input**.\n  - Green arrows indicate **Output**.\n  - Flame icon indicates **Trainable**.\n  - Snowflake icon indicates **Untrainable**.\n\n- **Lower training procedure panels**\n  - **Image FM** panel: shows **DINO V2** with **Student Image Transformer** and **Teacher Image Transformer**, fine-tuned using datasets labeled **TCGA**, **GTEx**, and **In-house**, producing an Image FM.\n  - **ST FM** panel: shows a **Single Cell FM** fine-tuned using **HEST1K** to produce a **Spatial Transcriptomics FM**.\n\nNo quantitative results or empirical findings are shown; the figure presents the architecture and training workflow."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 4, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel label: **c.**\n\nTitle: **H2O Results Analysis and Applications**\n\nVisible subpanels:\n- **Context-aware gene-expression prediction**: schematic heatmap/table with blue intensity values and row/column blocks; visible gene labels include **EPCAM**, **ACTA2**, and **n_genes**.\n- **Multi-modal informative clustering**: scatterplot-style clusters of colored points, grouped by dashed/outlined regions, representing separated cell/sample groups.\n- **Cell-cell communication**: network graph with connected nodes and edges; several nodes/regions are highlighted with translucent red circles.\n- **Temporal continuity**: line plot with a magenta curve increasing over time-like x-axis values; y-axis tick labels visible up to about 25.\n- **Spatial contiguity**: gray 3D tissue/block-like volume rendering.\n- **Multi-omics integration**: two spatial omics images labeled **Spatial Transcriptome** and **Spatial Proteome**; the transcriptome view is a circular spot/hex-like map with colored points, and the proteome view is a microscopy-like cellular image with red/green staining and overlaid circular markers.\n\nBiological/source objects visible: gene-expression matrix, genes/cell features, cell clusters, cell-cell interaction network, tissue volume, spatial transcriptomics spots, spatial proteomics/cellular microscopy image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces shown: prediction from contextual gene-expression data, multimodal clustering, communication network inference/visualization, temporal trajectory/continuity curve, spatial 3D/tissue contiguity representation, and integration of spatial transcriptome with spatial proteome data.\n\nFindings conveyed visually: H2O outputs can support gene-expression prediction, identify multimodal clusters, infer cell-cell communication, preserve temporal and spatial structure, and integrate spatial multi-omics modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 7, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure comparing spatial gene expression prediction methods.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panels labeled `a`, `c`, `d`, `e`, `f`, and `g`.\n- The figure appears from a bioRxiv preprint, with header text including DOI `10.64898/2026.04.21.717342`, posted April 24, 2026.\n\nPanel `a`:\n- Three boxplot groups showing `PCC` distributions.\n- Datasets/tissues labeled:\n  - `ccRCC`\n  - `PRAD`\n  - `IDCLymphNode (cross-dataset validation)`\n- Methods compared on x-axes:\n  - `H2O`\n  - `OmiCLIP`\n  - `DeepPT`\n  - `HistoGene`\n  - `BLEEP`\n- H2O generally has higher median PCC than the competing methods in these visible plots.\n\nPanel `c`:\n- Horizontal bar charts titled `Best Gene Prediction Count`.\n- Same three datasets/tissues: `ccRCC`, `PRAD`, `IDCLymphNode`.\n- Methods: `H2O`, `OmiCLIP`, `DeepPT`, `HistoGene`, `BLEEP`.\n- Visible counts:\n  - ccRCC: H2O `974`, OmiCLIP `398`, DeepPT `267`, HistoGene `170`, BLEEP `50`.\n  - PRAD: H2O `1271`, OmiCLIP `185`, DeepPT `170`, HistoGene `97`, BLEEP `60`.\n  - IDCLymphNode: H2O `1193`, DeepPT `228`, BLEEP `202`, OmiCLIP `185`, HistoGene `114`.\n\nPanel `d`:\n- Line/scatter comparison titled `Comparison of PCC between H2O and the best competing result`.\n- Y-axis: `PCC`.\n- Legend:\n  - red: `H2O`\n  - gray: `Best Except H2O`\n- Gene labels highlighted in blue include `SLC30A4`, `B2M`, `TMA7`, `MAGT1`, `G3BP2`, `ATP2C1`, `HLA-B`, `PRDX3`, `SMS`, and `PPDPF`.\n- H2O red points frequently exceed the gray competing baseline.\n\nPanel `e`:\n- Line plot titled `Top gap genes between H2O and competing methods`.\n- Y-axis: `PCC Gap`.\n- X-axis: `Gene`.\n- Many gene labels are shown vertically, including visible labels such as `CD38`, `MDH1`, `CACYBP`, `B2M`, `ATP5F1`, `HLA-B`, `CYTOR`, `PRDX3`, `CD24`, `PSMA3`, `NDUFA11`, `SLCA?`, `CPNE1`, and others.\n- The plotted gap decreases from roughly `0.42` toward about `0.225`.\n\nPanel `f`:\n- Horizontal bar chart of enriched terms with x-axis `-log10(P-value)`.\n- Terms include:\n  - `Epithelial, Ovary`\n  - `Trophectoderm, Embryo`\n  - `Embryonic Stem, Germ`\n  - `Epithelial, Ovarian Cortex`\n  - `Mesenchymal Stromal`\n  - `Microglial, Embryonic`\n  - `Mesenchymal Stem`\n  - `Sertoli, Fetal Gonad`\n  - `Plasmacytoid Dendritic, Blood`\n  - `Lonocyte, Lung`\n- P-values are annotated next to bars.\n\nPanel `g`:\n- Spatial expression map grids comparing `Ground Truth` against predicted outputs from:\n  - `H2O`\n  - `HistoGene`\n  - `BLEEP`\n  - `DeepPT`\n  - `OmiCLIP`\n- Two separate tissue/sample map groups are visible.\n- Gene columns on the left group:\n  - `KRT18`\n  - `KRT8`\n  - `CDH1`\n  - `EPCAM`\n- Gene columns on the right group:\n  - `CD38`\n  - `DSP`\n  - `CDH1`\n  - `STEAP2`\n- Color scale labeled `Normalized Expression`, ranging from `0.0` to `1.0`, with green at low values and red at high values.\n- Biological source objects shown are tissue-section spatial maps, likely histology-derived spatial transcriptomics samples, but no specific tissue identity is stated in panel `g` beyond the dataset labels elsewhere.\n\nOverall finding visible from the figure:\n- The model/interface named `H2O` is compared against `OmiCLIP`, `DeepPT`, `HistoGene`, and `BLEEP` for gene expression prediction.\n- H2O appears to achieve higher PCC distributions, the largest number of best-predicted genes, and closer-looking spatial expression maps for several marker genes in the shown examples."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about spatial transcriptomics / spatial domain recognition and cell clustering, with model comparison and downstream ligand-receptor analysis.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- Panel a: Spatial domain recognition maps for three methods/inputs labeled `ST`, `Image FM`, and `H2O`. Each shows a tissue-shaped spatial spot map colored by cluster/domain labels, with small legends numbered `0–3`.\n\n- Panel b: Horizontal bar plots comparing methods. One plot shows `H2O` around `0.66` versus `Image_FM` around `0.34`. A second plot shows `H2O` around `0.63` versus `Image_FM` around `0.44`. Axes include `Methods`; metric labels are not visible in the crop.\n\n- Panel c: Cell clustering maps for `ST`, `Image FM`, and `H2O`. Tissue spatial maps are colored by multiple clusters, with legends including labels up to about `8`.\n\n- Lower boxed section under panel c: `t-SNE map`, showing three low-dimensional scatterplots with colored clusters corresponding to the clustering outputs.\n\n- Large right-side boxed comparison: Three rows labeled `H2O Reproduced`, `H2O Newly Discovered`, and `H2O Regrouped`. Each row contains paired t-SNE-like scatterplots with grey background points and highlighted colored subsets. Legends show cluster labels, including `0–8` depending on subplot.\n\n- Panel e: Spatial tissue grid/network visualization. Top shows a tissue-shaped array of spots with colored regions. Bottom contains zoomed callouts: one enlarged tissue spot grid with colored interaction-like radial line patterns, and another close-up network diagram. The close-up labels ligand-receptor directions: `L: MIF, R: CD44` in green and `L: MIF, R: CD74` in blue.\n\n- Panel f: Left shows a spatial tissue map colored by many components on a dark/black background. Right shows a stacked bar chart of ligand-receptor record counts by interaction pair, with legend titled `component` and many numbered components. A text box reports:\n  - `No. of ligands: 198`\n  - `No. of ligand and/or receptor genes: 371`\n  - `No. of ligand receptors: 1036`\n\n- Panel g: Bar chart labeled `Pattern Abundance (#)`, ranking ligand-receptor pattern combinations. Visible labels include interactions involving `MIF_CD44`, `MIF_CD74`, `COPA_CD74`, `CXCL12_CXCR4`, `VEGFA_FLT1`, and others.\n\nBiological/source objects visible: spatial tissue sections represented as spot grids or spatial coordinates; cell/spatial clusters; ligand-receptor interaction pairs such as MIF-CD44 and MIF-CD74.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces visible: comparison of `ST`, `Image FM`, and `H2O`; spatial domain recognition; cell clustering; t-SNE embeddings; reproduced/newly discovered/regrouped H2O cluster views; ligand-receptor/component analysis.\n\nFindings shown visually: `H2O` appears to outperform `Image_FM` in the bar plots; H2O produces more detailed or differently segmented spatial/cell clusters; downstream analysis identifies many ligand-receptor records and highlights prominent MIF-related interaction patterns."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 12, "caption": "made available under a CC-BY-NC 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.21.717342; this version posted April 24, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic scientific workflow. It shows a developmental timeline with stylized embryo/fetus/infant stages along a horizontal blue arrow. Downward arrows point from several stages to narrow vertical tissue-section-like samples. One stage is labeled “Delivery.”\n\nA rightward arrow leads to a large water droplet icon labeled “H2O,” containing stylized red tissue/cell structures in the upper part and blue dot patterns in the lower part, suggesting a processing or model/input interface. Another rightward arrow leads to stacked spatial grid/heatmap panels with blue and gray squares.\n\nThe final output is labeled “Predicted ST Expression,” indicating prediction of spatial transcriptomics expression from developmental or tissue-section inputs. No quantitative findings, gene names, specific organism, tissue identity, or model name are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 12, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a scientific plot labeled panel **“b.”** with the title **“Pearson of Genes from Cytokines.”**\n\nVisible content:\n- A grouped boxplot comparing **PCC** values across genes.\n- Y-axis label: **PCC**.\n- X-axis label: **Gene**.\n- Genes shown include cytokine-related genes such as **CCL19, CCL2, CCL4, CCL5, CXCL8, CXCL1, CXCL10, CXCL14, CXCR3, IL1B, IL1RN, IL3, IL7R, SPP1**.\n- Legend at upper right: **Age**, with two groups:\n  - dark blue: **fet**\n  - light gray: **paed**\n- Each gene has paired boxplots comparing the two age/source groups.\n- The plotted data appear to represent Pearson correlation coefficients for gene-level cytokine measurements or associations.\n\nVisible findings:\n- **CCL19** has the highest PCC values among the displayed genes in both groups.\n- Several genes show higher median PCC in the **paed** group than the **fet** group, including visually apparent cases such as **CXCL10, CXCL14, CXCR3, IL7R, and SPP1**.\n- Outlier points are visible for some genes, including around **CCL5**.\n- No model architecture, imaging modality, microscopy image, or biological specimen morphology is shown; this is a statistical summary plot."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 14, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel `a` shows a schematic workflow. It starts with a “Series of histopathology images,” drawn as multiple microscopy slide-like image planes containing brown tissue regions. An arrow points to “Registration,” shown as stacked tissue section images with pink tissue areas aligned along a dotted vertical axis. Another arrow points to a water-drop icon labeled “H2O,” containing stylized pink cellular/tissue shapes above blue dot patterns. A final arrow points to “Predicted 3D tissue profiles,” represented as a pixelated 3D cube or volumetric grid with blue and gray blocks.\n\nVisible labels: `a.`, “Series of histopathology images,” “Registration,” “H2O,” and “Predicted 3D tissue profiles.”\n\nBiological source objects: histopathology tissue images/sections, represented as tissue regions on slides and registered pink tissue slices.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: serial histopathology images undergo image registration, then pass through an H2O-labeled computational/model step, producing predicted 3D tissue profiles.\n\nFinding/output shown: a predicted 3D tissue profile volume derived from registered histopathology image series."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 14, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a multi-panel 3D scientific visualization labeled “e.” with three side-by-side volumetric renderings. Each panel displays a cube-like 3D structure with axes labeled X Axis, Y Axis, and Z Axis, with numeric tick marks.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left: labeled “LYZ”\n- Middle: labeled “LYZ + ACTA2”\n- Right: labeled “LYZ + ACTA2 + SPP1”\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The figure appears to visualize spatial biological structures or tissue-derived volumetric data with marker/gene labels LYZ, ACTA2, and SPP1.\n- The rendered objects are irregular 3D segmented volumes or point/voxel-based surfaces.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The panels show progressive overlay or combination of marker-associated spatial structures:\n  - LYZ alone\n  - LYZ combined with ACTA2\n  - LYZ combined with ACTA2 and SPP1\n- The rightmost panel includes teal/green highlighted regions over gray/white volumetric structures, suggesting an additional marker or segmented component associated with SPP1 or the combined marker set.\n\nFindings visible:\n- Adding ACTA2 changes or expands the visible dark/gray volumetric regions relative to LYZ alone.\n- Adding SPP1 introduces teal-highlighted spatial regions distributed across parts of the 3D structure.\n- The figure conveys spatial co-localization or combined marker distribution in a 3D biological volume."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows two labeled panels, `h.` and `i.`, containing UMAP scatter plots from an immune-cell analysis.\n\nPanel `h.` includes three side-by-side UMAP embeddings:\n- `Immune RNA Clusters`: cells plotted in UMAP1/UMAP2 space and colored by RNA-derived cluster labels `0` through `7`.\n- `Immune Protein Clusters`: the same style of UMAP visualization, colored by protein-derived cluster labels `0` through `5`.\n- `Immune WNN Clusters`: UMAP visualization colored by weighted-nearest-neighbor cluster labels `0` through `7`.\n\nPanel `i.` shows:\n- `Immune WNN Protein Weight`: a UMAP scatter plot colored by a continuous protein-weight value from `0.0` to `1.0`, using a viridis-like color scale.\n\nBiological source objects: individual immune cells represented as points in low-dimensional UMAP space.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces shown: RNA-based clustering, protein-based clustering, weighted nearest neighbor clustering, and WNN-derived protein modality weighting.\n\nVisible finding: RNA, protein, and WNN clustering assign different cluster structures across the immune-cell UMAP manifold, while the WNN protein weight varies spatially across the embedding, indicating regions where protein information contributes more or less strongly."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing UMAP scatter plots comparing RNA and protein marker expression across immune cell phenotypes.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Two rows labeled `RNA` and `Protein`.\n- Five columns labeled:\n  - `T Helper`\n  - `Cytotoxic T`\n  - `Regulatory T`\n  - `Activated T`\n  - `B Cell`\n- Each panel is a UMAP embedding with axes labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`.\n- Each column uses a marker label above the plot:\n  - T Helper: `CD4`\n  - Cytotoxic T: `CD8A`\n  - Regulatory T: `FOXP3`\n  - Activated T: `CD38`\n  - B Cell: `CD40`\n- Each panel has a colorbar indicating marker expression intensity.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single cells or cell populations embedded in UMAP space.\n- Immune cell marker genes/proteins associated with T cell and B cell subsets.\n- RNA expression markers and matched protein-level marker measurements.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- High-dimensional single-cell measurements are projected into 2D UMAP coordinates.\n- Marker expression values are overlaid as colored points on the same UMAP layout.\n- RNA and protein modalities are compared visually by row.\n\nVisible findings:\n- RNA marker expression and protein marker expression differ in spatial patterns and dynamic ranges.\n- CD4, CD8A, FOXP3, CD38, and CD40 show modality-specific localization across the UMAP embedding.\n- Protein panels generally show stronger localized intensity patterns for several markers compared with the RNA panels."]}]

