e Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\", \"grounding_valid\": true, \"picture_only_provenance\": false, \"id\": \"InputRouteDiscovery_be66612d2f9a5292\", \"route_label\": \"Continued-pretraining text stream\", \"model_name\": \"Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507\", \"lifecycle_phase_verbatim\": \"continued pretraining\", \"task_or_configuration_verbatim\": \"RVQ-text paired descriptions\", \"source_object_verbatim\": \"biomedical literature\", \"transformation_chain_verbatim\": [\"BPE tokenizer\"], \"model_visible_form_verbatim\": \"BPE tokens\", \"insertion_or_fusion_verbatim\": \"fused at the LLM layer\", \"text_role_verbatim\": \"actual input\", \"input_status_verbatim\": \"actual model input\", \"evidence_quote\": \"interleaved with biomedical literature\", \"section_heading\": \"3.4 Multi-Stage Training Pipeline\", \"supporting_figure_or_table\": null, \"uncertainty\": \"The paper describes the CPT corpus at the mixture level; this route isolates the text side of that paired sequence.\", \"grounding\": {\"pages\": [12], \"doc_item_refs\": [\"#/texts/334\", \"#/texts/335\", \"#/texts/336\", \"#/texts/337\", \"#/texts/338\", \"#/texts/339\"], \"provenance_match\": \"verbatim\"}}, {\"route_ref\": \"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121::route_006\", \"record_id\": \"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121\", \"paper_title\": \"RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\", \"grounding_valid\": true, \"picture_only_provenance\": false, \"id\": \"InputRouteDiscovery_72a183f99abcffa3\", \"route_label\": \"Continued-pretraining RVQ stream\", \"model_name\": \"Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507\", \"lifecycle_phase_verbatim\": \"continued pretraining\", \"task_or_configuration_verbatim\": \"RVQ-text paired descriptions\", \"source_object_verbatim\": \"single-cell gene expression profiles\", \"transformation_chain_verbatim\": [\"RVQ tokenizer\"], \"model_visible_form_verbatim\": \"RVQ discrete codes\", \"insertion_or_fusion_verbatim\": \"embedded directly into the LLM vocabulary\", \"text_role_verbatim\": \"paired training supervision\", \"input_status_verbatim\": \"actual model input\", \"evidence_quote\": \"The training data consists of RVQ-text paired descriptions\", \"section_heading\": \"3.4 Multi-Stage Training Pipeline\", \"supporting_figure_or_table\": null, \"uncertainty\": \"The paper describes continued-pretraining inputs in paired form rather than diagramming the RVQ stream separately.\", \"grounding\": {\"pages\": [12], \"doc_item_refs\": [\"#/texts/334\", \"#/texts/335\", \"#/texts/336\", \"#/texts/337\", \"#/texts/338\", \"#/texts/339\"], \"provenance_match\": \"verbatim\"}}]\n\nRecord metadata:\n{\"record_id\": \"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121\", \"title\": \"RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\", \"doi\": \"10.64898/2026.04.20.719773\"}\n\nComplete canonical Docling Markdown:\n## RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nGuangpeng Li 1,2,† , Yue You 1,† , Yunlin Fu 1 , Weige Zhou 1,3 Feng Tang 1 , Jiaming Kong 4, ∗ , Luyi Tian 1,3, ∗\n\n1 Guangzhou National Laboratory, Guangzhou, China 2 Electronic Information Engineering Program, School of Computer Science and Engineering, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, China 3 GMU-GIBH Joint School of Life Sciences, Guangzhou Medical University, Guangzhou, China 4 Rectified Flow Capital Limited, Hong Kong SAR, China\n\n#### Abstract\n\nIntegrating continuous single-cell gene expression profiles with discrete-token large language models (LLMs) remains an open challenge: text-based methods are token-inefficient and discard quantitative precision, continuous embeddings preclude autoregressive generation, and single-codebook vector quantization cannot stratify biological scales.\n\nWe present RVQ-Alpha , an end-to-end framework that bridges this modality gap through three contributions:\n\n(i) a Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ) tokenizer that compresses each cell into a fixed 10-token sequence via eight residual codebooks embedded directly in the LLM vocabulary, requiring 3.4 × fewer tokens than prior discrete methods and enabling bidirectional unification of cell interpretation and generation;\n\n(ii) scCoT-Synth , a teacher-student engine that grounds newly added biological tokens through evidence-before-conclusion reasoning, using the language modeling objective as the cross-modal alignment signal without a separate projection network;\n\nand (iii) a Fact-Aware RLVR system combining an ontology-grounded answer judge with saliency-weighted verification of biological claims against actual expression data, under dynamic gating that conditions hallucination suppression on task competence.\n\nBuilt on Qwen3-4B and trained via continued pretraining, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning, RVQ-Alpha substantially improves out-of-distribution generalization and rare-cell recognition across eight held-out datasets; ablations confirm that evidence-first grounding reduces hallucination more than fivefold.\n\n∗ † Equal contribution; ∗ Corresponding authors: ⟨ kongjiaming@renaissance-era.com ⟩ , ⟨ tian\\_luyi@gzlab.ac.cn ⟩\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nBuilding computational models that understand and simulate cellular behavior, a vision termed the AI Virtual Cell [1], is a central goal of computational biology. Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) [2] provides the high-resolution transcriptomic data required by such models, and large language models (LLMs) offer powerful reasoning and generation capabilities that could serve as their computational substrate [3, 4]. Realizing this vision demands a model that can both interpret existing cell states (e.g., cell type annotation, disease classification) and generate new cell states (e.g., perturbation simulation) within a unified architecture. We address a specific setting: compressing continuous gene expression profiles into discrete tokens that reside directly in the LLM vocabulary, enabling a single autoregressive model to achieve both the understanding and generation of cellular representations.\n\nThis problem is particularly challenging because it requires simultaneously preserving quantitative expression information, achieving token efficiency, and enabling autoregressive generation through the LLM vocabulary.\n\nSingle-cell foundation models have scaled rapidly (from scGPT [5] with 33M cells and Geneformer [6] with 30M cells, to CellFM [7] with 100M cells and 800M parameters, and scPRINT-2 [8] with 350M cells across 16 organisms); yet, systematic benchmarks show that no single model dominates across all downstream tasks (Tables 8 and 9) [9, 10, 11]. More fundamentally, these continuous-representation methods [5, 6, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18] learn cell embeddings through masked expression prediction; they produce representations that enable accurate classification but cannot be decoded back through an LLM vocabulary . This architectural limitation precludes autoregressive cell generation and prevents integration with natural language reasoning chains.\n\nTo overcome this vocabulary incompatibility, text-based approaches convert expression profiles into natural language that pre-trained LLMs can process directly, using either ranked gene lists [19, 20, 21, 22, 23] or structured textual descriptions enriched with biological knowledge [24, 25, 26, 27]. However, these approaches introduce a different fundamental trade-off: ranked gene lists discard quantitative expression precision and produce token-inefficient representations (40-80 tokens per cell for the top-20 genes), whereas structured descriptions scale poorly to population-level multi-cell reasoning [28]. Recent work has further enhanced text-based LLMs with reasoning capabilities (for example, CellReasoner [29] introduces chain-of-thought annotation from 380 exemplars, and Cello1 [30] applies reinforcement learning with batch-level rewards). Nevertheless, these approaches still operate on text-based gene representations and lack mechanisms to verify biological claims against actual expression data .\n\nA concurrent line of work explores discrete tokenization to resolve this trade-off. CellTok [31] demonstrates that vector-quantized variational autoencoder (VQ-VAE) discrete tokens, integrated into the Qwen2.5 vocabulary via early fusion, enable previously unattainable population-level analyses. However, CellTok employs a single shared codebook that cannot allocate capacity across biological scales; furthermore, it does not incorporate structured reasoning and relies solely on supervised fine-tuning without reinforcement learning. The core technical challenge remains: how to achieve discrete tokenization that preserves multi-scale biological structure, combined with verifiable reasoning that grounds predictions in actual gene expression evidence .\n\nWe propose RVQ-Alpha , an end-to-end framework that resolves this challenge through three integrated innovations. Figure 1 illustrates the overall framework. Our core innovation is a Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ) autoencoder that compresses the expression profile of each cell into eight discrete tokens drawn from eight residual codebooks of 32 entries each (8 × 32 = 256 codes). This yields a variance-ordered decomposition that tracks cellular identity from broader lineage distinctions to finer functional states (a correspondence we validate empirically in §6), requiring approximately 60% fewer tokens than text-based representations. Because these tokens reside directly in the LLM vocabulary, the model can both interpret RVQ sequences for analytical tasks and generate new RVQ sequences for cell state simulation, thereby achieving bidirectional unification .\n\nFigure 1: Overview of RVQ-Alpha. Top: An RVQ Encoder discretizes gene expressions, followed by a progressive Pretrain-SFT-RL alignment with an LLM to enable multi-modal reasoning. Bottom: The unified model drives diverse downstream applications, spanning single-cell, population, and perturbation analyses.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic describing an RVQ-Alpha model for single-cell data and downstream biological tasks.\n\nPanel a shows the main workflow and model architecture. On the left, a “Data Encoding Pipeline” converts “scRNA-seq Data” through an “RVQ Encoder” into “Discrete Tokens,” then into “Multi-Task Instruction.” In the center, the “RVQ-Alpha Model” is shown as a circular training loop labeled “Pretrain & SFT & RL,” with schematic AI/model icons. On the right, an “MLLM Architecture” box connects “Diverse Single Cell Data” to two components labeled “LLM” and “RVQ,” which feed into “Capabilities” including “Understanding,” “Generation,” and “Reasoning.”\n\nPanels b-d show “Downstream tasks.” Panel b is labeled “Single-Cell Task” and includes cell type identification, top-K gene identification, gene expression generation, and batch effect correction. Panel c is labeled “Cell Population Task” and includes homogeneous cell type identification, heterogeneous cell type identification with cell type matching at instance level, and homogeneous phenotype identification. Panel d is labeled “Cell Perturbation Task” and includes gene expression change prediction and differential expressed genes prediction, using pre-perturbation cell population state, perturbation details such as drug name and concentration, target gene name, and outputs such as upregulated/downregulated changes or differential gene lists.\n\nBiological source objects shown include scRNA-seq data, diverse single-cell data, cell populations, cell types, gene expression vectors, genes, perturbation conditions, drug perturbations, target genes, and differential expression outputs. The figure depicts transformations from single-cell molecular data into discrete RVQ tokens and instruction-style inputs, then into multimodal language-model components for prediction, generation, reasoning, and downstream single-cell analysis tasks.\n\nHowever, newly added RVQ tokens carry no biological semantics, creating a cold-start problem for this LLM-native integration scheme. To address this, we develop Evidence-First Feature-Injection SFT , which provides the topK expressed genes alongside RVQ tokens and enforces an evidencebefore-conclusion reasoning structure. This design forces the language modeling loss to align RVQ hidden states with gene-level semantics, using the training objective itself as an implicit alignment available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning signal. To further improve reasoning quality beyond what supervised learning alone can achieve, we construct a multi-component RLVR reward system [32] that evaluates answer accuracy via an LLM-as-judge approach [33] and assesses reasoning quality across five dimensions (independence, robustness, causality, granularity, and consistency). Furthermore, a Fact Verification module decomposes reasoning chains into atomic biological claims and validates each against the actual expression data of the cell. This expression-grounded verification extends the RLVR paradigm recently explored for biological reasoning [34] by providing fine-grained, cell-specific reward signals.\n\nWe evaluate RVQ-Alpha on a held-out pool of eight evaluation corpora curated from the ScBank singlecell collection (11,914 single-cell test samples spanning 12 tissues, five diseases, and 106 cell types), using disjoint training, validation, and test partitions (§4.5). Compared to supervised fine-tuning baselines, RLVR training yields consistent improvements: accuracy on the held-out immune-cell evaluation set improves from 70.7% to 82.8% (+12.1 pp), rare neuroblastoma recognition rises from 36.1% to 65.0% (+28.9 pp), population-level cell-type classification accuracy increases from 16.8% to 78.0% (+61.2 pp), and COVID-19 disease-state prediction accuracy doubles to 66.2%.\n\nOur main contributions are as follows:\n\n- We introduce RVQ discrete tokenization for single-cell transcriptomics, marking the first application of residual vector quantization to gene expression data. An eight-codebook residual quantizer (with 32 entries each) produces a representation of 10 tokens per cell embedded natively in the LLM vocabulary (§3.2), requiring 60% fewer tokens than text-based approaches (Table 8). Furthermore, empirical analysis (§6) demonstrates that earlier codebooks predominantly encode broader identity levels, whereas later codebooks refine within-lineage variation.\n- We develop scCoT-Synth , an evidence-grounded supervision engine that overcomes the coldstart problem of newly added biological tokens without requiring a cross-modal projector. By coupling constraint-conditioned rationale synthesis with a seven-layer verification stack, this engine aligns discrete token representations with gene-level semantics entirely through the language modeling loss.\n- We construct a multi-component RLVR reward system with Fact Verification that validates biological claims against actual expression data. This approach addresses the hallucination problem inherent in LLM-native biological tokenization, yielding up to a 4.6 × improvement on population-level tasks (Table 9).\n\n## 2 Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Single-Cell Foundation Models with Continuous Representations\n\nThe first generation of single-cell foundation models employed custom transformer architectures operating directly on continuous gene expression data.\n\nscGPT [5] trains a generative transformer on 33 million cells via masked gene expression prediction, thereby supporting multi-task fine-tuning for annotation, integration, and perturbation prediction.\n\nGeneformer [6] utilizes rank-value encoding over 30 million cells to learn network hierarchies through attention weights.\n\nscBERT [12] adapts BERT with binning-based discretization for cell-type annotation.\n\nSubsequent efforts have continued to scale: scFoundation [13] (100M parameters, 50M cells), CellFM [7] (800M parameters, 100M cells, RetNet architecture), and scPRINT-2 [8] (350M cells across 16 organisms).\n\nAlternative architectures have also emerged. CellPLM [14] treats cells as tokens and tissues as sentences, integrating spatial transcriptomics. GeneMamba [35] replaces transformers with Mamba for available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning computational efficiency. AIDO.Cell [15] scales dense representations to 650M parameters to handle the full 20K-gene transcriptome. UCE [16] employs protein language model (ESM2) embeddings for zeroshot cell mapping. Nicheformer [17] handles both dissociated and spatial data, while SCimilarity [18] learns metric-learning embeddings for scalable atlas search.\n\nDespite this rapid progress, systematic benchmarks [9, 10, 11] reveal that no single foundation model dominates across all tasks. Simple logistic regression can outperform large transformer models, and tokenization choices-rather than model scale-are decisive under distribution shift [28]. Crucially, all these approaches produce continuous cell embeddings that cannot be decoded through an LLM vocabulary, fundamentally limiting their integration with natural language reasoning.\n\n### 2.2 Language Models for Single-Cell Biology\n\nAparallel line of research incorporates general-purpose LLMs into single-cell analysis by converting expression data into text.\n\nCell2Sentence [19] pioneered this direction by transforming expression profiles into ranked gene 'sentences' for GPT-2 fine-tuning, an approach later scaled to 27B parameters [20].\n\nCELLama [21] applies sentence transformers to ranked gene lists with metadata integration.\n\nscMulan [22] encodes genes and expression values as entity-value tuples using multi-task prompting.\n\nLangCell [24] and CellHermes [25] develop cross-modal pretraining frameworks that unify text and expression within shared representation spaces.\n\nInstructCell [27] constructs an instruction-following copilot for single-cell tasks, while CellWhisperer [26] combines Geneformer embeddings with BioBERT via CLIP-like training for chat-based exploration.\n\nStack [36] trains on 149M cells using tabular attention with in-context learning capabilities.\n\nAlthough these text-based approaches benefit from pre-trained language understanding, they face fundamental trade-offs. Ranked gene lists discard quantitative precision and scale poorly, as representing even 20 genes requires 40-80 tokens. More critically, text-based representations preclude autoregressive cell generation : the model cannot produce new cell states by generating tokens that map back to expression profiles.\n\n### 2.3 Discrete Tokenization for Biological Data\n\nVector quantization converts continuous signals into discrete codes that LLMs can process natively.\n\nVQ-VAE [37] introduced the quantized autoencoder framework for images.\n\nResidual Vector Quantization, employed in neural audio codecs [38, 39], applies sequential codebooks to residuals, capturing multi-scale structure through progressive refinement.\n\nIn biology, Sun et al. [40] apply geometric byte-pair encoding for protein structure tokenization.\n\nMost relevant to our work, CellTok [31] concurrently demonstrated VQ-VAE discrete tokenization for early-fusion integration with Qwen2.5. They showed that discrete cell tokens enable population-level analyses previously unattainable with text-based approaches. However, CellTok employs a single shared codebook-a design that cannot allocate capacity across biological scales-and produces a 34-token per-cell footprint, which is 3.4 × longer than the representation we propose in §3.2.\n\nRVQ-Alpha differs from CellTok in three key aspects: (1) we use Residual VQ, a stack of codebooks in which each successive codebook quantizes the residual of its predecessors, with §6 presenting available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning empirical evidence of a coarse-to-fine correspondence with cellular hierarchy; (2) we develop EvidenceFirst chain-of-thought synthesis for interpretable reasoning; and (3) we introduce RLVR with fact verification for post-SFT policy optimization.\n\n### 2.4 Reasoning and Reinforcement Learning for Biological LLMs\n\nRecent work has begun exploring reasoning-enhanced LLMs for single-cell biology.\n\nCellReasoner [29] fine-tunes a 7B LLM with 380 chain-of-thought exemplars for marker-by-marker reasoning during cell-type annotation, thereby achieving strong few-shot performance.\n\nCell-o1 [30] applies reinforcement learning with batch-level rewards to single-cell reasoning puzzles, demonstrating a 73% improvement over OpenAI's o1 on batch-level annotation accuracy.\n\nOmniCellAgent [41] employs multi-agent orchestration for autonomous single-cell analysis and hypothesis generation.\n\nBeyond the single-cell domain, the RLVR paradigm has been applied to broader biological reasoning.\n\nDeepSeek-R1 [32] demonstrated that verifiable rewards improve LLM reasoning by margins comparable to several months of pretraining scaling.\n\nrbio1 [34] trains scientific reasoning LLMs using biological world models as 'soft verifiers,' setting the state of the art on the PerturbQA benchmark.\n\nVCWorld [42] integrates structured biological knowledge with LLM reasoning for interpretable perturbation prediction.\n\nRVQ-Alpha builds on these developments by combining discrete tokenization (enabling bidirectional generation), Evidence-First reasoning (grounding predictions in gene evidence), and RLVR with a Fact Verification module that validates claims against actual expression data. This integrated approach addresses the hallucination risk inherent in LLM-native biological tokenization.\n\n### 2.5 Perturbation Prediction\n\nPredicting cellular responses to perturbations is a core task for virtual cell models.\n\nCPA [43] learns factorized perturbation embeddings with adversarial disentanglement.\n\nGEARS [44] integrates gene-gene knowledge graphs for multigene perturbation prediction.\n\nDiffusion-based approaches such as Squidiff [45] model perturbation responses through continuous denoising.\n\nRVQ-Alpha addresses perturbation prediction through a distinct mechanism: RVQ tokens enable the model to autoregressively generate post-perturbation cell states, producing discrete token sequences that can be decoded back to expression profiles via the RVQ decoder.\n\n## 3 Method\n\nRVQ-Alpha integrates four components into an end-to-end framework for single-cell transcriptomic reasoning: (i) a residual-quantization cell tokenizer that embeds gene expression into the native token vocabulary of the LLM (§3.2), (ii) a scCoT-Synth pipeline that constructs evidence-first chain-of-thought training data (§3.3), (iii) a three-stage training pipeline progressing from token grounding through multi-task SFT to reinforcement learning (§3.4), and (iv) a multi-teacher distillation and fact-aware reward system that unifies multi-task capabilities while suppressing biological hallucinations (§3.6-3.8). Figure 1 provides a system overview.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n### 3.1 Problem Formulation\n\nInputs and outputs. Let x ∈ R G denote a single-cell gene expression profile with G ∈ [ 2,000, 36,602 ] measured genes, and let P = { x 1 , . . . , x N } ( N ∈ [ 10, 100 ] ) denote a cell population. Each sample is paired with an instruction t specifying the task. The target output y is either (i) a text response (such as a categorical label for cell type, tissue, or disease, a ranked gene list, or a CoT reasoning trace) or (ii) a sequence of RVQ discrete codes encoding a predicted post-perturbation expression profile.\n\nTask families. RVQ-Alpha addresses seven task families spanning single-cell and population levels (Table 1).\n\nTable 1: Task families supported by RVQ-Alpha. All single-cell tasks emit a CoT reasoning rationale alongside the answer.\n\n| Task Family                     | Input   | Output                                                                        | Level       |\n|---------------------------------|---------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------|\n| Cell-type identification        | x , t   | Cell-type label                                                               | Single-cell |\n| Tissue identification           | x , t   | Tissue label                                                                  | Single-cell |\n| Disease-state identifica- tion  | x , t   | Disease-state label                                                           | Single-cell |\n| Marker-gene identifica- tion    | x , t   | Ranked gene list                                                              | Single-cell |\n| Expression-profile gener- ation | x , t   | RVQ-encoded expression profile                                                | Single-cell |\n| Perturbation prediction         | P , t   | Predicted post-treatment RVQ codes and implied differentially expressed genes | Population  |\n| Population comparison           | P , t   | Per-cell annotation and population-level summary                              | Population  |\n\nThree-stage optimization. Training proceeds through three stages with a composite objective:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere L RVQ trains the cell tokenizer (§3.2), L SFT performs multi-task supervised fine-tuning (§3.4), and L RLVR applies reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (§3.7). Each stage builds strictly on the checkpoint produced by its predecessor.\n\n### 3.2 Residual Quantization for LLM-Native Cell Tokens\n\nTokenizing cell data for an LLM involves balancing competing requirements.\n\nAper-cell footprint short enough to fit tens of cells alongside instruction text in one context window demands aggressive compression, whereas preserving the continuum of cellular identity (from broad lineage distinctions to fine-grained functional states) requires expressive capacity.\n\nAthird constraint compounds the first two: cell tokens must enter the LLM through its native token embedding table rather than through a separately learned cross-modal adapter, which would relegate gene expression to a secondary modality and add a trainable bottleneck between cell identity and the reasoning path.\n\nExisting designs fail to optimally resolve this tradeoff.\n\nText-based encodings such as Cell2Sentence [19] consume 100-500 tokens per cell, thereby precluding population-scale prompts.\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nFlat VQ-VAE tokenizers such as CellTok [31] (following the original VQ-VAE design [37]) compress a cell into 32 codes drawn from a single shared codebook; however, a single codebook cannot allocate capacity across biological scales, and the resulting representation of 34 tokens per cell is still 3.4 × longer than necessary.\n\nRVQ-Alpha addresses all three requirements with a residual-quantized tokenizer whose codes are embedded directly into the vocabulary of the LLM as a fixed representation of 10 tokens per cell.\n\nResidual quantization. Aparametric encoder f enc maps gene expression to a D -dimensional latent z = f enc ( x ) .\n\nGiven Nc codebooks E k = { e ( j ) k } C j = 1 ⊂ R D , the tokenizer quantizes z through successive residual approximation:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwith a symmetric decoder producing ˆ x = f dec ( ˆ z ) and end-to-end training under standard RVQ objectives (Supplementary A.5).\n\nWe deploy Nc = 8 codebooks of C = 32 entries each, a configuration that outperforms alternative ( Nc , C ) choices on held-out reconstruction and clustering metrics (§6.1).\n\nTwo structural properties follow directly from the residual recursion.\n\nFirst, each codebook is fitted to the residual left by its predecessors; optimization therefore drives earlier codebooks to explain the highest-variance directions of the latent space, leaving progressively smaller residuals for later codebooks to refine.\n\nSecond, this variance-ordered decomposition permits, though does not guarantee, a correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity, where lineage typically dictates the primary axes of transcriptomic variation and functional states refine variation within lineages.\n\nWe therefore state as a testable hypothesis, rather than an architectural property, that earlier codebooks predominantly encode lineage-defining features, whereas later codebooks encode cell-type-specific and state-specific signatures; we validate this empirically in §6.\n\nVocabulary-native integration. RVQ-Alpha exposes cell codes to the LLM as first-class vocabulary items: the Nc × C grid of codes is enumerated into a contiguous block of token identifiers, embedded through the same token embedding table used for text tokens, without any additional cross-modal adapter.\n\nConcretely, each code pair ( k , c k ) is assigned the unique identifier\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere V text is the text vocabulary size of the base LLM.\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nFigure 2: Core dual-tokenization architecture. Text instructions are processed by a BPE tokenizer (top), while gene expression data passes through the RVQ tokenizer (bottom). Both token streams are fused at the LLM layer ( p ( t | h , c ) ), enabling bidirectional encoding and decoding: the BPE and RVQ detokenizers reconstruct the original modalities from the unified representation.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow figure for tokenizing and modeling single-cell data with LLM-style discrete tokens.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Two parallel input streams are shown on the left:\n  - **Meta data**, represented as small document-like panels.\n  - **Expression data**, represented as multiple single-cell study plots labeled with studies such as “Study 1,” “Study 2,” “Study 3,” and “Study-N.”\n- The metadata stream passes through a blue block labeled **“BPE Tokenize”**, producing a **Words Vocabulary** example with tokens such as “Nanocells,” “are,” “ultra-small,” “cells,” “~10nm,” and “diameter.”\n- The expression data stream passes through an orange block labeled **“RVQ Tokenize”**, producing a colored grid labeled **“Single Cell codebook.”**\n- Both streams are converted into vertical sequences labeled as discrete tokens. The lower caption reads **“Single Cell Discrete Tokens.”**\n- A central model block labeled **“LLMs”** receives token sequences and shows a next-token prediction style formula/interface, indicating language-model processing over mixed metadata and expression-derived tokens.\n- On the right, generated or decoded token sequences pass through:\n  - **“BPE Detokenize”** for metadata, producing text output: “Nanocells are ultra-small cells.”\n  - **“RVQ Detokenize”** for expression data, reconstructing study-like expression data panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell expression data from multiple studies.\n- Associated metadata text describing biological/cellular concepts.\n\nTransformations:\n- Text metadata is transformed into BPE tokens and later detokenized back into text.\n- Single-cell expression data is transformed into RVQ discrete tokens via a single-cell codebook and later detokenized back into expression data.\n\nModel interface:\n- A central LLM operates on the combined discrete token sequences from metadata and expression data.\n\nVisible finding/claim:\n- The figure illustrates a multimodal tokenization framework where both textual metadata and single-cell expression data are represented as discrete tokens that can be modeled by LLMs and decoded back into their original modalities.\n\nThis process adds Nc × C = 256 cell-code tokens; furthermore, per-cell and population-level delimiters separate cells and cell groups, yielding a fixed representation of 10 tokens per cell (eight codes plus two per-cell delimiters).\n\nThis size is 3.4 × smaller than the 34-token representation of CellTok [31] (32 codes + 2 delimiters) and 10-50 × smaller than the text-serialized gene lists of Cell2Sentence [19] (100-500 tokens for the top-ranked genes); all counts exclude shared instruction-level prompt tokens.\n\nThis compression enables population-level reasoning with tens of cells in a single context window.\n\nThe resulting dual-path pipeline, in which text flows through the BPE tokenizer and gene expression flows through the residual-quantized tokenizer before fusing at the shared embedding layer, is shown in Figure 2; embedding initialization and vocabulary-expansion details are provided in Supplementary A.5.\n\n### 3.3 scCoT-Synth: Evidence-Grounded Supervision Engine for Cold-Start RVQ Alignment\n\nThe 256 RVQ tokens introduced during vocabulary expansion carry no pretrained semantics. This cold-start grounding problem compounds the reasoning-format challenge of DeepSeek-R1 [32] with a cross-modal dimension.\n\nscCoT-Synth addresses this issue by requiring every CoT training trajectory to decode RVQ codes into verifiable gene evidence before reasoning. Consequently, the standard next-token prediction loss forces the RVQ embeddings toward the gene-name representations already encoded in the pretrained weights.\n\nThe resulting engine produces approximately 600K training samples covering 46 task variants that span the seven task families.\n\nEvidence-first constraint. Every CoT training sample follows a directed evidence-reasoning chain:\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere c = ( c 0, . . . , c 7 ) denotes the RVQ codes and t denotes the task instruction.\n\nThe model must produce verifiable gene evidence E (e.g., 'elevated CD8A , CD8B , GZMB ; absent CD4 ') before any reasoning step R .\n\nBecause gene-name tokens in E are predicted from RVQ input positions via the attention mechanism, the cross-entropy loss on these tokens provides a dense supervisory signal that pulls RVQ embeddings toward the pretrained gene-name representations, without requiring any explicit alignment module.\n\nScaffolded teacher-student synthesis. The constraint is realized through a teacher-student protocol with asymmetric information.\n\nDuring teacher synthesis , a replaceable LLM backend receives curated gene features (such as expressionranked genes, known markers, active transcription factors, and functional gene modules) alongside c and t , enabling biologically accurate evidence-first reasoning.\n\nA conclusion-blind elicitation protocol prevents the teacher from short-circuiting this process. Although the teacher receives metadata labels for context anchoring, the system prompt constrains it to derive conclusions solely from gene evidence, reasoning as if discovering them for the first time. Deterministic validators flag outputs that reference labels directly. For continued-pretraining data, the teacher regenerates these flagged samples; for CoT data, the flag is recorded as a non-blocking quality signal.\n\nDuring student training , gene features are stripped entirely, and the input to the student contains only c ⊕ t .\n\nGene names survive solely in the assistant response (the training target) as evidence claims that the student must learn to predict from the RVQ codes alone.\n\nThe gene features thus act as scaffolding : they are present during construction to ensure teacher accuracy and absent during training to force genuine RVQ grounding.\n\nTask-grounded generation at scale. Grounding RVQ tokens requires exposure across various biological granularities, including individual cell profiles, cell populations, and extended expository contexts, because a single granularity would bias the learned representations toward that specific level of description.\n\nscCoT-Synth therefore comprises three generation tiers: (i) cell-level CoT generation across five annotation tasks (cell-type classification, tissue classification, disease classification, gene-name reconstruction, and expression-profile generation); (ii) population-level CoT across five tasks operating on groups of up to ∼ 50 cells; and (iii) audience-diversified continued pretraining drawn from a combinatorial space defined by four audience profiles, nine stylistic templates, and up to 108 variants along four content dimensions (analysis depth, reasoning path, gene organization, and data format).\n\nAll tiers share the core design principles of teacher scaffolding and conclusion-blind elicitation, adapted to the output format of each tier.\n\nMulti-layer verification. Every generated trajectory passes through a verification stack spanning deterministic structural checks, rubric-based semantic scoring, and RVQ arithmetic validation (Table 2).\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 2: Verification stack. Layers 1-5 are enforced during data construction; Layers 6-7 apply across all pipelines. † Non-blocking quality signal recorded with the CoT sample but never used to discard it. ‡ Blocking criterion for continued-pretraining samples; failures are regenerated by the teacher.\n\n|   L | Name                     | Method                        | Enforcement        |\n|-----|--------------------------|-------------------------------|--------------------|\n|   1 | Context constraint       | System-prompt behavioral rule | Soft †             |\n|   2 | Evidence-first structure | Regex position check          | Soft † / Hard ‡    |\n|   3 | Forbidden reference      | Phrase filter                 | Soft † / Hard ‡    |\n|   4 | Reasoning-conclusion     | Regex label extraction        | Soft †             |\n|   5 | LLM-as-judge rubric      | Weighted multi-dim rubric     | Soft (scored)      |\n|   6 | RVQ code arithmetic      | Offset + range validation     | Blocking criterion |\n|   7 | Nomenclature norm.       | Curated mapping rules         | Blocking criterion |\n\nThe LLM-as-judge (Layer 5) evaluates CoT samples across four dimensions (evidence grounding, logic rigor, factual accuracy, and uncertainty calibration), utilizing a separate five-dimensional profile for continued-pretraining data. 2\n\nThis verification stack yields the final corpus of approximately 600K quality-assessed SFT samples across the 46 task variants.\n\nWhy it works: gradient pathway for cross-modal alignment. The evidence-first structure creates a gradient pathway from gene-name prediction positions back to RVQ embeddings via the attention value path:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere AE indexes gene-evidence token positions and α i , j is the attention weight from position i to RVQ position j .\n\nWithin each layer, the gradient on e RVQ j is modulated by the attention mass assigned to that code at gene-prediction positions. This mechanism represents one of several gradient pathways that together provide a cross-modal supervisory signal without requiring an explicit mapping layer.\n\nAblation studies confirm this effect: removing evidence-first generation degrades accuracy by 8.3,pp and increases hallucinations from 4.2% to 23.7% (§6).\n\nThe resulting corpus of evidence-grounded trajectories (comprising CoT chains and augmented narratives) provides the initial SFT supervision for cold-start RVQ alignment.\n\nThe structured output format, where gene evidence E precedes reasoning R , enables the downstream Fact-Aware RLVR module (§3.8) to decompose predictions into atomic biological claims for expressiongrounded reward verification.\n\n### 3.4 Multi-Stage Training Pipeline\n\nAs noted in §3.3, the 256 RVQ tokens introduced by vocabulary expansion carry no pre-existing semantics in the language model: their embeddings are randomly initialized and bear no learned associations with existing representations in the model.\n\n2 Reasoning profile weights: evidence grounding, = 0.30, logic rigor, = 0.35, factual accuracy, = 0.25, uncertainty calibration, = 0.10; each dimension is scored from 1 to 5 and normalized to [ 0, 100 ] .\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nDirect fine-tuning on task-specific data therefore faces a cold-start problem , because the model cannot extract meaningful information from RVQ inputs until these tokens are semantically grounded.\n\nEven after grounding, supervised fine-tuning alone imposes a ceiling on output quality. The model can only reproduce reasoning patterns present in the training data and cannot self-correct factual or logical errors without outcome-based feedback.\n\nThese constraints motivate a three-stage pipeline (Figure 1).\n\nStage 1 (Continued Pretraining) grounds the new RVQ tokens in the representation space of the language model.\n\nStage 2 (Multi-Task SFT) teaches structured instruction following and CoT reasoning across seven single-cell task families.\n\nStage 3 (RLVR via GSPO) refines reasoning quality and factual reliability through verifiable reward signals (§3.7).\n\nAcross all three stages, the upstream RVQ encoder f enc remains frozen; only the LLM parameters are updated.\n\n###### Stage 1: Continued Pretraining\n\nThe goal is to ground the 256 newly introduced RVQ tokens in the pre-trained representation space of the language model prior to any task-specific supervision.\n\nVocabulary expansion adds five structural delimiters and 256 RVQ tokens. These are initialized under a diversity-promoting scheme ensuring that the new embeddings are geometrically dispersed rather than clustered in the representation space (full procedure in §3.2).\n\nAll parameters of the language model, including the newly added token embeddings, are jointly optimized under a standard next-token prediction objective.\n\nThe training data consists of RVQ-text paired descriptions spanning diverse linguistic registers (nine template families; §3.3), which are interleaved with biomedical literature and explicitly cover the top 500 gene names.\n\nUnlike conventional continued pretraining with pure text, Stage 1 data already adopts an instructionresponse format. This enables task-oriented co-exposure : the model simultaneously learns RVQ semantics and the prompt structure of downstream tasks, thereby reducing the distributional gap to Stage 2.\n\nThe resulting checkpoint yields a model that maps RVQ tokens to meaningful representations but lacks structured task-following behavior, which motivates the multi-task supervision of Stage 2.\n\n###### Stage 2: Multi-Task SFT\n\nThe goal of Stage 2 is to teach the model structured instruction following and chain-of-thought reasoning across seven single-cell task families.\n\nThe Stage 1 checkpoint is fine-tuned on the full scCoT-Synth corpus. Each training example carries a task-family annotation that selects the appropriate input formatting and loss masking for one of the seven task families.\n\nStage 2 performs full fine-tuning of the language-model backbone while keeping the upstream RVQ encoder frozen.\n\nThe loss is restricted to assistant token positions, ensuring that the model learns to generate responses rather than memorize prompts:\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere A indexes assistant tokens, t denotes the task instruction, and f enc ( x ) represents the RVQ cell encoding.\n\nDuring training, sampling weights across task families are adjusted to prevent any single dataset from dominating the gradient signal.\n\nHowever, the output quality of the model remains bounded by the training distribution, which motivates the reward-driven optimization of Stage 3.\n\n###### Stage 3: RLVR via Group Sequence Policy Optimization\n\nThe goal of Stage 3 is to refine reasoning quality and factual reliability beyond the SFT ceiling through reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards.\n\nWe adopt Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO) [46], which operates at the sequence level rather than the token level (§3.5).\n\nIts asymmetrically tight clipping bounds serve as the sole trust region, eliminating the need for an explicit KL penalty.\n\nFor each prompt q , the policy samples Ng candidate responses, each of which is scored by the composite reward signal (§3.7).\n\nRollout prompts are drawn from the same task families as in SFT, and group-normalized advantages (§3.5) provide a per-task baseline that adapts to heterogeneous reward distributions.\n\nFor multi-task scenarios, the Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (MOPD) protocol (§3.6) coordinates Stage 3 by first training task-specific experts through independent GSPO runs, and subsequently distilling their capabilities into a unified student.\n\nThe Stage 3 checkpoint constitutes the final RVQ-Alpha model evaluated in §4.\n\n### 3.5 Group Sequence Policy Optimization\n\nBiological reasoning tasks in Stage 3 require response-level evaluation: the correctness of a cell-type prediction or perturbation rationale depends on the complete answer rather than on any individual token.\n\nGroup Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO) [46] addresses this requirement by operating at the sequence level , in contrast to the earlier Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) [47].\n\nGroup-normalized advantages. For each prompt q , the policy samples Ng candidate outputs { yi } Ng i = 1 and computes group-normalized advantages:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere R ( yi , y ∗ ) represents the composite reward (§3.7). This group-relative baseline is shared with GRPO; the key differences lie in the importance ratio and the clipping regime.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 3: GRPO [47] vs. GSPO [46]. Both algorithms share the group-relative advantage estimator (Eq. 10); however, they differ in the unit of the importance ratio, the clipping scope and magnitude, and the handling of the KL penalty.\n\n|                                                                      | GRPO (Shao et al., 2024)                              | GSPO (Zheng et al., 2025)                                                                                                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Importance ratio Clipping unit Clip range KL penalty Group advantage | token-level per token ε ≈ 0.2 explicit per-token term | sequence-level s i (Eq. 11) per response ε low = 3 × 10 - 4 , ε high = 4 × 10 - 4 omitted; tight clip provides the trust region identical (Eq. 10) |\n\nSequence-level importance ratio. Unlike GRPO, which computes a separate importance ratio for each token position, GSPO defines a single sequence-level ratio per response as the geometric mean of the per-token ratios:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nAsymmetric clipped surrogate. The policy is optimized using an asymmetric clipped surrogate objective:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwith asymmetric bounds ε low = 3 × 10 -4 and ε high = 4 × 10 -4 , following the values reported by Zheng et al. [46] for Qwen3-30B-A3B-Base. These bounds are two orders of magnitude tighter than the per-token bounds of GRPO ( ε low = 0.2, ε high = 0.27) reported in the same paper, because sequencelevel ratios exhibit a significantly smaller dynamic range. No per-token Kullback-Leibler (KL) penalty is applied; the tight clip alone provides the trust region.\n\nRelationship between GSPO and MOPD. GSPO serves as the shared base RL algorithm across all training configurations. The Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (MOPD) protocol (§3.6) wraps GSPO rather than replacing it. Specifically, in MOPD Stage A, each task expert independently runs GSPO on its designated task family; subsequently, in MOPD Stage C, the student runs GSPO under additional multi-teacher KL constraints. This separation keeps the policy optimization algorithm fixed while MOPDaddresses the orthogonal problem of unifying multi-task capabilities.\n\n### 3.6 MOPD: Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation\n\nMotivation. Joint multi-task RLVR trained with a single mixed reward signal exhibits a persistent see-saw effect : reward gains on cell-type identification consistently degrade perturbation prediction accuracy, and vice versa. The root cause is gradient interference across heterogeneous task families whose reward landscapes are geometrically conflicting. Standard remedies are insufficient; mixedreward RLVR cannot simultaneously reach per-task peaks, and post-hoc parameter merging suffers from catastrophic forgetting. Inspired by MiMo-V2-Flash [48] and Baichuan-M3 [49], we introduce MOPD, a separate-then-unify protocol adapted for single-cell multi-task reasoning. Figure 3 provides empirical evidence for this design choice.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nFigure 3: Specialized vs. mixed RL training. Each panel compares task-specific (blue) and mixed multi-task (red) RLVR training. Specialized training consistently outperforms mixed training across all four tasks, with the gap widening after step 300. Homogeneous cell type shows the largest relative improvement (+192.5%), motivating the MOPD separate-then-unify protocol.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a four-panel scientific line plot comparing LLM judge accuracy over training steps for two model conditions: a blue solid “Specialized model” and a red dashed “Mixed model.” Each panel shows smoothed accuracy curves with scattered point observations, shaded blue regions under the specialized-model curve, gridlines, and annotated peak or notable values.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: “Cell Type (Homogeneous)”\n- Top right: “Tissue Type (Homogeneous)”\n- Bottom left: “Disease State (Homogeneous)”\n- Bottom right: “Cell Type (Heterogeneous)”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- X-axis in all panels: “Training Step”\n- Y-axis in all panels: “LLM Judge Accuracy (mean@1)”\n- Accuracy is shown as percentages.\n- Legend at top center: blue solid line = Specialized model; red dashed line = Mixed model.\n\nBiological/source objects represented:\n- Biological metadata prediction or classification categories: cell type, tissue type, and disease state.\n- Homogeneous and heterogeneous dataset/task settings are compared.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, genes, or molecular structures are directly shown; the figure is entirely quantitative performance curves.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The plot appears to track model performance during training.\n- It compares specialized training against mixed-model training across biological annotation tasks.\n- Smoothed trend lines are overlaid on raw scatter points.\n\nFindings visible in the figure:\n- Specialized models generally improve with training and often outperform mixed models.\n- Cell Type (Homogeneous) shows strong improvement for the specialized model, reaching an annotated value around 0.840, while the mixed model remains much lower.\n- Tissue Type (Homogeneous) shows both models closer together early, but the specialized model reaches an annotated value around 0.640.\n- Disease State (Homogeneous) shows smaller gains, with specialized and mixed curves closer than in other panels.\n- Cell Type (Heterogeneous) shows the specialized model improving substantially over the mixed model, with an annotated specialized value around 0.383.\n- Each panel includes a small annotation box comparing specialized vs mixed performance, with visible relative gains such as +192.5%, +15.7%, +10.3%, and +83.3%.\n\nStage A: Task-specific expert training. Starting from the shared Stage 2 SFT checkpoint π 0, we train K domain-specific expert models { π ( k ) } K k = 1 independently. Each expert runs GSPO on its designated task family:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere D ( k ) and r ( k ) represent the domain-specific dataset and reward, respectively. The expert set comprises three task-specialized experts (one each for cell-type identification, perturbation prediction, and population-level reasoning) alongside a generalist trained on the union of all task families. The core philosophy is differentiation before unification : by removing cross-task interference, each expert converges to its per-domain performance peak.\n\nStage B: Offline policy distillation. During this stage, all teachers are frozen. Each teacher π ( k ) generates a trajectory dataset D ( k ) traj . The student πθ learns from ⋃ k D ( k ) traj via the Clip-Forward-KL objective:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nwhere π t is the domain-appropriate teacher. The indicator I [ · ] enforces student non-inferiority only where the teacher is superior, without demanding exact distributional matching. The mode-covering property of Forward-KL ensures that the student broadly spans the high-probability regions of all expert distributions, establishing a generalist floor .\n\nStage C: Multi-teacher on-policy distillation. The distilled student re-enters an online RLVR environment. Stage C switches from Forward-KL to Reverse-KL , which is mode-seeking rather than mode-covering; consequently, the student actively selects the optimal behavior per prompt instead of averaging across teacher modes. The MOPD surrogate loss incorporates an importance-sampling (IS) correction:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere µθ is the behavior policy (the old snapshot), and the truncated IS weight and token-level teacher advantage are given by:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere sg [ · ] denotes the stop-gradient operator and π dom ( x ) denotes the expert teacher assigned to the task family of prompt x (from Stage A). Discrepancy masking (where wt = 0 outside [ ε low , ε high ] ) provides stable IS correction. The final per-token advantage combines the dense teacher signal with a sparse outcome reward:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere A ORM is the outcome reward model (ORM) advantage (shared across all tokens). This two-legged structure leverages complementary signals: A MOPD t provides stable per-token guidance on process quality, while A ORM enforces task correctness at the sequence level.\n\nDual-path teacher signal. Each teacher contributes through two complementary channels: a tokenlevel path supplying log π dom ( x ) ( yt | x , y &lt; t ) for fine-grained Reverse-KL constraints, and a sequence-level path providing a holistic score over the complete response for global coherence. These two paths target process quality and outcome quality, respectively.\n\nKL regime shift and iterative co-evolution. The transition from Forward-KL (Stage B, modecovering) to Reverse-KL (Stage C, mode-seeking) is deliberate: offline Forward-KL broadly absorbs all expert knowledge, whereas online Reverse-KL concentrates on the single best mode per prompt. The unified student from Stage C can subsequently initialize a new round of Stage A, creating a self-reinforcing co-evolution cycle that supports continuous improvement in multi-task capabilities.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n### 3.7 Ontology-Grounded Reward Design\n\nOntology-aware semantic evaluation. Exact-match rewards are ill-suited to biological cell type prediction, where valid names coexist at different levels of the Cell Ontology [50] hierarchy.\n\nB cell' and naive B cell' are both correct descriptions of the same population at different specificity levels; T helper cell' and CD4-positive T cell' name the same entity in different nomenclatures.\n\nUnder exact-match scoring, a model that correctly identifies a naive B cell but outputs the parent term B cell' receives zero reward (identical to a prediction of neuron'), collapsing the entire ontological hierarchy into a binary signal that actively penalizes valid predictions.\n\nThe reward must therefore capture graded semantic similarity anchored to ontological distance, rather than reducing biological correctness to string identity.\n\nAfrozen LLM judge addresses this by evaluating the semantic relationship between prediction ˆ y and ground truth y ∗ with explicit Cell Ontology awareness. The rubric below defines the reward-side answer judge , which is distinct from the post-training offline evaluation judge used to report accuracy (§4.5); its category schema and multi-sample aggregation are properties of the training-time reward system only.\n\nThe reward-side answer judge classifies responses into seven graded categories reflecting positions on the ontology graph, ranging from Exact Match and More Specific (valid sub-type) through Less Specific (valid ancestor) to Wrong Branch , Vague/Evasive , Wrong Lineage , and Unrelated , spanning a continuous score in [ -1, 1 ] (scoring intervals are detailed in Appendix A.4).\n\nThe overall reward pipeline is depicted in Figure 4.\n\nMultiplicative gating. The continuous judge score is decomposed into positive and negative components, s + ans = max ( 0, s raw ) and s -ans = min ( 0, s raw ) , which enter the reward formula through disjoint paths.\n\nRather than a simple additive combination R = α R ans + β R reason, which allows a model to earn positive reward through plausible reasoning atop an incorrect answer, RVQ-Alpha employs a piecewise multiplicative gate :\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere w base , w reason, and w neg are gating weights; P rule , P fmt , and P ans are non-positive penalty terms; and the final score is clamped to [ -1, 1 ] .\n\nThree properties of this design are critical.\n\nFirst, the hard gate : when the answer is wrong ( s + ans = 0), the reasoning score is entirely excluded; no amount of reasoning quality can compensate for an incorrect answer.\n\nSecond, the max ( 0, s reason ) clamp ensures that only genuinely constructive reasoning contributes a boost; neutral or negative reasoning yields zero rather than penalizing a correct answer.\n\nThird, asymmetric penalty flow : the negative answer signal s -ans always propagates regardless of format validity, ensuring that the model cannot escape penalty by producing deliberately malformed output.\n\nFor population-level tasks, each of N cells receives an independent judge evaluation, and the positive and negative components are aggregated via the mean before entering the same gate (Eq. 19); population-specific consistency penalties and the aggregation formula are detailed in Appendix A.4.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow diagram for an evaluation or judging pipeline. It contains multiple labeled panels connected by arrows and dashed routing lines.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “Input” with two columns labeled “answer” and “metadata,” illustrated with document and database icons.\n- Middle upper panel: “Rule & Safety Guard,” containing three components:\n  - “Hard Constraints”\n  - “Prompt Injection Defense”\n  - “Rule Penalties”\n- Right upper panel: “Dual-Judge Semantic Engine,” with:\n  - “Answer Judge,” shown as a gauge/meter\n  - “Conditional Switch”\n  - “Reasoning Judge,” with bar-chart subdimensions labeled “Independence,” “Robustness,” “Sufficiency,” “Granularity,” and “Consistency”\n- Lower dashed-box pipeline:\n  - “Template Matching,” including “Predict Label,” “Regex Filter,” and “Scanner”\n  - “Semantic Mapping Engine,” comparing “Predict Label” and “Ground Truth” with categories “Exact Match,” “Vague/Directional,” and “Unrelated”\n  - “Electronic Switch,” with “Threshold Slider” and “Trigger Condition Judge Score > Threshold”\n  - “Multi-Dimensional Audit Hub,” with sliders for “Independence (30%),” “Robustness (25%),” “Sufficiency (20%),” “Granularity (15%),” and “Consistency (10%),” followed by “De-Biasing Audit & Dimension Weighting” and “Reasoning Quality Score”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The diagram shows an input answer/metadata flow through rule and safety checks, then into a dual-judge semantic engine.\n- A lower branch performs template matching and semantic mapping against ground truth.\n- Conditional/electronic switches route scores into a weighted multi-dimensional audit module.\n\nFindings shown:\n- No experimental results are shown. The figure presents a system architecture for rule filtering, semantic judging, label matching, and weighted reasoning-quality scoring.\n\nFigure 4: Ontology-grounded reward pipeline. Model outputs first pass through rule and safety guards, then enter a dual-judge semantic engine comprising an ontology-aware Answer Judge and a conditional Reasoning Judge (top). The lower panels detail template matching, semantic mapping, conditional switching, and multi-dimensional reasoning audit modules that implement label extraction, semantic grounding, gating, and weighted reasoning-quality scoring. Defense layer details are in Appendix A.4.\n\nTwo binary pre-gates and additional safeguards enforce format compliance and guard against reward exploitation; specifications, including a dominance constraint ensuring net-negative reward for structural abuse, are in Appendix A.4.\n\nConditional reasoning evaluation. The reasoning judge is invoked only when the answer is at least partially correct and both pre-gates pass. This avoids providing a training signal for 'how well the model reasons about incorrect conclusions,' as such a signal would encourage plausible but wrong available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning reasoning chains.\n\nWhen invoked, the judge evaluates the CoT chain across five weighted dimensions:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThe two most heavily weighted dimensions are Independence ( d ind ), which measures whether conclusions derive from gene evidence rather than restating the query, and Counterfactual Robustness ( d rob ), which tests whether reasoning holds under different tissue or disease contexts. These two dimensions are the most diagnostic of genuine biological reasoning versus superficial pattern matching.\n\nThree additional dimensions complete the rubric: Causal Sufficiency, Granularity Discipline, and Consistency (full definitions and weights are provided in Appendix A.4).\n\nAsixth dimension, Label Semantic Match , is logged for monitoring but excluded from the reward to avoid double-counting with the answer judge.\n\nTogether, the ontology-aware answer judge and the gated reasoning rubric cover semantic correctness and reasoning structure, but neither inspects the factual content of intermediate claims, which motivates the layer developed next.\n\n### 3.8 Fact-Aware Reinforcement Learning\n\nThe ontology-grounded reward described above ensures prediction correctness, but a correct answer produced through flawed reasoning (such as citing fabricated gene markers or hallucinating pathway associations) represents a distinct failure mode that the answer judge cannot detect.\n\nHallucination-accuracy trade-off. Standard hallucination-penalized RLVR using a count-based ratio R = R task + α · R hallu suffers from two failure modes: (i) redundancy-induced dilution , where the policy outputs many correct but uninformative sentences to inflate the denominator and suppress the apparent hallucination rate; and (ii) penalty-induced conservatism , where a large α drives the policy toward terse outputs that sacrifice the multi-step reasoning required for biological inference.\n\nAn effective fact reward must therefore weight claims by their informational importance to defeat dilution, while modulating its influence based on the current task competence of the model to avoid premature conservatism.\n\nInspired by Baichuan-M3 [49], RVQ-Alpha employs a saliency-weighted, dynamically gated factverification reward adapted for single-cell biological reasoning.\n\nSaliency-weighted fact reward. Each reasoning chain is decomposed into independently verifiable atomic claims, which are evaluated against domain-specific knowledge bases covering cell-type markers, pathway memberships, and disease-gene associations, and subsequently classified as SUPPORTED, REFUTED, or UNCERTAIN.\n\nEach representative claim c ∗ k is assigned a saliency weight that measures the maximum cosine similarity between its embedding and the embeddings of the response sentences. The weighted fact reward captures this variation in claim importance:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nThe denominator accumulates weighted information content rather than the raw claim count. Consequently, appending low-salience sentences barely increases it, whereas high-saliency refuted claims incur disproportionately large penalties, focusing the signal on core biological assertions.\n\nDetails regarding the claim extraction procedure, knowledge base inventory, and verification caching are provided in Appendix A.4.\n\nDynamic multi-objective aggregation. Afixed coupling between R task and R fact is counterproductive early in training, when the model has not yet learned to generate substantive claims worth verifying. RVQ-Alpha applies a soft gating coefficient:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere σ is the logistic sigmoid function, κ controls the transition steepness, µ is the inflection point, and ∆ represents a temperature scale.\n\nThe final composite reward combines task performance with dynamically gated factual accuracy:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThis formulation partitions the training process into three regimes:\n\n- a Protection Zone ( R task &lt; τ min, λ ≈ 0) where the task reward dominates;\n- a Transition Zone ( τ min ≤ R task ≤ τ max) where factual constraints increase nonlinearly;\n\nand a Constraint Zone ( R task &gt; τ max, λ ≈ 1) where full hallucination suppression is engaged.\n\nTogether with the ontology-grounded task reward, this implements a two-layer verification curriculum: the model must first demonstrate semantic correctness before factual-accuracy constraints engage. The empirical effects of this progression are examined in Section 4.\n\n## 4 Experimental Setup\n\n### 4.1 Base Model\n\nRVQ-Alpha uses a single base LLM across all training stages and task families: Qwen3-4B-Thinking2507 [51] (4.0,B parameters, V text = 151,941), extended with Nc · C = 8 × 32 = 256 RVQ single-cell vocabulary slots, five structural delimiter tokens, and the top-500 gene name tokens (tokenizer details in §3.2).\n\nThe Thinking variant was selected because our RLVR objective explicitly rewards the reasoning trace (§3.7), aligning the native reasoning behavior of the model with the downstream training objective.\n\n### 4.2 Training Protocol\n\nWe train RVQ-Alpha in three sequential stages: continued pretraining (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR). The pipeline begins from the single backbone introduced above; each subsequent stage initializes from the merged checkpoint produced by the unified checkpoint-selection procedure described at the end of this subsection. Training uses sixteen NVIDIA H20 96,GB GPUs in total: four GPUs each for CPT and SFT, and eight GPUs for each RLVR run, with an additional eight GPUs hosting the LLM judge. Table 4 summarizes the per-stage configuration.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 4: Training-protocol summary. Stage-wise training settings for the three-stage pipeline. Full hyperparameter grids, the checkpoint-merging configuration, and prompt templates are deferred to the appendix.\n\n| Item                | CPT                                    | SFT                                     | RLVR (per run)                             |\n|---------------------|----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| Base checkpoint     | Qwen3-4B + 256 RVQ to- kens            | Merged CPT checkpoint                   | Merged SFT checkpoint                      |\n| Training data       | ≈ 225,000 records, six- source mixture | ≈ 121,000 records, five- source mixture | 28-200 records per task                    |\n| Split granularity   | Donor-level; 8 held-out donors         | Same as CPT                             | Same donor pool                            |\n| Sequence length     | 16,384 packed                          | 16,384 packed (prompt cap 4,096)        | 4,096 prompt + 16,384 re- sponse           |\n| Effective batch     | 2 seq / 32,768 tok                     | 2 seq / 32,768 tok                      | 16 prompts × 8 rollouts = 128 trajectories |\n| Optimizer / peak LR | Fused AdamW / 5 × 10 - 5               | Fused AdamW / 1 × 10 - 6                | AdamW / 1 × 10 - 6                         |\n| LR schedule         | Cosine / 10% warmup                    | Cosine / 20% warmup                     | Cosine / 10 warmup steps (0.5%)            |\n| Total updates       | 13,000                                 | ≈ 7,000-15,000 (2 epochs)               | 2,000 RLVR optimizer updates               |\n| Compute             | 4 × H20 96,GB                          | 4 × H20 96,GB                           | 8 train + 8 judge H20 96,GB                |\n| Stage output        | Merged CPT checkpoint                  | Merged SFT checkpoint                   | Merged RLVR check- point                   |\n\nContinued pretraining. CPT extends the base model on a six-source mixture of ≈ 225,000 records combined in natural proportions (with no per-source weighting). The evaluation is held out at the donor level on eight donors (A013, D094-D096, D099, D101-D103) that are disjoint from the training set by construction.\n\nEach 16,384-token packed sequence is sharded across GPUs using fully sharded data parallelism with a (data-parallel, context-parallel, tensor-parallel) degree configuration of ( 2, 2, 1 ) .\n\nOptimization uses AdamW at a peak learning rate of 5 × 10 -5 with a 10% cosine warmup and a weight decay of 0.1; computation uses BF16 mixed precision with FP32 accumulation for gradient reduction and FP32 optimizer states.\n\nTraining covers 13,000 optimizer updates at an effective global batch size of 2 sequences (32,768 tokens per step); the resulting ≈ 4.26 × 10 8 training tokens correspond to approximately one to two effective epochs over the corpus.\n\nSupervised fine-tuning. SFT initializes from the merged CPT checkpoint and refines it on ≈ 121,000 chat-template conversations with chain-of-thought reasoning drawn from five sources, inheriting the CPT donor-held-out split.\n\nThe loss is computed exclusively on assistant spans under the Qwen3 chat template.\n\nSFT is deliberately light-touch: we decrease the peak learning rate by two orders of magnitude to 1 × 10 -6 and raise the warmup ratio from 0.1 to 0.2 to smooth the transition from the peak learning rate of CPT.\n\nParallelism, precision, sequence length, and effective batch size match those of CPT; the run covers two epochs at 32,768 tokens per step.\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nRLVR. RLVR initializes from the merged SFT checkpoint and covers three per-task runs: the homogeneous tissue task, the homogeneous disease-state task, and the heterogeneous cell-type task.\n\nWe adopt GSPO [46] on top of the GRPO advantage estimator [47], with the full objective deferred to §3.4.\n\nBecause the geometric-mean likelihood ratio of GSPO concentrates with response length, we tighten the clip bounds to 3 × 10 -4 /4 × 10 -4 (compared to the PPO default of 0.2) and disable all KL and entropy regularization.\n\nThe reward combines rule-based format and repetition checks with an LLM judge (Qwen3-30B-A3B, hosted on a separate inference server); the reward formula, judge rubric, and weights are specified in §3.7.\n\nEach step generates 128 trajectories (16 prompts × 8 rollouts) with a temperature of 1.0; training runs for 2,000 steps on eight GPUs with AdamW at a learning rate of 1 × 10 -6 (Table 4).\n\nUnified Checkpoint Protocol. Each stage selects its output using the same protocol: saving periodically (every 1,000 steps for CPT and SFT, and every five RLVR optimizer updates for RLVR), scoring every saved checkpoint on a fixed held-out benchmark, and averaging the top-5 checkpoints by score with uniform weights (linear weight averaging in parameter space). The resulting merged checkpoint serves as the stage output from which the subsequent stage initializes and which is evaluated in this paper. The ranking metrics differ by stage: CPT and SFT use held-out single-cell LLM-judge accuracy, while RLVR uses task-specific LLM-judge accuracy on the per-task validation set.\n\nAll reported results use a single seed (seed 42). The checkpoint ranking uses stochastic one-sample decoding ( T = 0.6, topp = 0.95, topk = 20); individual scores therefore carry sampling noise that the top-5 merge attenuates. Donor disjointness between training and evaluation is strictly enforced for the homogeneous cell-type task and maintained as an operating convention for the remaining population tasks. The mixed-precision configuration (BF16 compute, FP32 gradient accumulation, FP32 AdamW moments) is summarized in the appendix. Full hyperparameter grids, the checkpointmerging configuration, the RLVR training hyperparameters, and prompt templates are deferred to the supplementary material.\n\n### 4.3 Datasets\n\nCorpus identity. We draw pretraining and supervised fine-tuning data from the ScBank repository of donor-level human scRNA-seq profiles.\n\nOur working corpus is a 55-file indexed donor-level subset comprising 31,481,661 cells from approximately 45 source study families (the provenance index is released with the repository).\n\nThe indexed corpus spans 101 standardized tissues , 103 disease states , and 859 standardized cell-type labels (Table 5).\n\nThese counts describe the harmonized metadata space of the corpus and exceed the effective class counts of any individual benchmark task (§4.4).\n\nOf the 55 dataset IDs, 53 are traceable to upstream ScBank source records; the remaining two are documented in our corpus index without a corresponding catalog match.\n\nMetadata harmonization. Tissue, disease, and cell-type annotations were canonicalized through fieldalias normalization and dictionary-based value mapping before any downstream task construction.\n\nBecause metadata completeness varies across files (for instance, the donor identifier and tissue are present in all 55 files, whereas disease, cell type, sex, and age coverage is partial, as shown in Table 5), RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 5: Corpus summary: 55-file indexed subset of ScBank. Label counts reflect the harmonized metadata space of the indexed corpus, not the effective class counts of any downstream benchmark (cf. Table 6). †\n\n| Property                    | Value                                        |\n|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| Indexed files               | 55                                           |\n| Total cells                 | 31,481,661                                   |\n| Source study families       | ≈ 45 distinct upstream studies               |\n| Standardized tissues        | 101                                          |\n| Standardized disease states | 103                                          |\n| Standardized cell types     | 859                                          |\n| Catalog traceability        | 53/55 IDs linked to upstream catalog entries |\n\n† Core metadata fields (donor ID, tissue) are present in all 55 files; disease and cell type in 54; sex in 50; age in 42.\n\nwe characterize the corpus by its harmonized label space rather than by a uniformly populated metadata schema.\n\nQCand preprocessing. The corpus supports metadata-level claims regarding label harmonization and retention, but it does not provide a fully reconstructable expression-matrix preprocessing pipeline, as standard per-cell quality control fields (such as gene count, total count, and mitochondrial fraction) are only sparsely populated across the processed files.\n\nAccordingly, we report only the metadata-supported harmonization here; the expression-to-token pipeline (binning, RVQ token assignment) is detailed in §3.2.\n\nTraining variants. Both the SFT and RL stages operate on task-specific JSONL instances derived from the indexed corpus.\n\nSingle-cell cell-type classification uses one of three training pools: a filtered pool of 98,873 cells (the default for the main text) and two larger unfiltered pools of 286,452 and 2,867,679 cells, which are used only where explicitly noted.\n\nUnless otherwise stated, the main-text results use the 98,873-sample filtered pool (Table 6).\n\nThe population task-pool counts in Table 6 refer to construction-pool sizes; the per-task subsampling for training is described alongside each task.\n\n### 4.4 Benchmarks\n\nDownstream evaluation is organized around two capability clusters: single-cell annotation at the individual-cell level, and population-level analysis over cohorts of 3-48 cells. Within each cluster, task targets are decomposed orthogonally from the population-construction mechanism: the supervision axis represents the label family (cell type, tissue, or disease), whereas the terms homogeneous and heterogeneous refer to how the internal composition of a population is assembled.\n\nSingle-cell benchmark. The single-cell benchmark evaluates cell-type generalization on eight heldout ScBank datasets (A013, D094-D096, D099, D101-D103) totaling 11,914 test samples across 12 tissues, five diseases, and 106 cell types (Table 6).\n\nAninth held-out ID (D097) is reserved for RVQ representation-boundary audits (§4.5) and does not appear in any main-text evaluation.\n\nPer-dataset accuracy and LCAD results on this eight-dataset pool are reported in §5.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 6: Benchmark task mapping. Training-pool counts are construction-pool sizes (§4.3). The default cell-type training pool is the 98,873-sample filtered variant; the 286,452-sample and 2,867,679-sample unfiltered pools are used only when explicitly stated. Label counts are the effective class counts per task and differ from the corpus-level counts in Table 5. '-' marks a task without a released evaluation artifact.\n\n| Cluster            | Task         | Input                           | Training pool                   | Eval set         |   Labels |\n|--------------------|--------------|---------------------------------|---------------------------------|------------------|----------|\n| Single-cell        | Cell-type ID | 1 cell                          | 98,873 (filtered)               | 11,914 / 8 files |      106 |\n| Pop. homogeneous   | Cell type    | 3-30 cells 3-15 cells           | 4,624 (pool) 45,421             | - 2,000          |       28 |\n| Pop. homogeneous   | Tissue       |                                 | (pool)                          | (sampled)        |       10 |\n| Pop. homogeneous   | Disease      | 1-40 cells                      | 49,427 (pool)                   | 2,000 (sampled)  |        5 |\n| Pop. heterogeneous | Cell type    | 16-48 cells, 2-8 types          | 225 (pool)                      | -                |       68 |\n| Pop. heterogeneous | Tissue       | 10-25 cells, 2-6 tissues        | 100 (pool)                      | -                |        2 |\n| Pop. heterogeneous | Disease      | Not included in this benchmark. | Not included in this benchmark. |                  |          |\n\nWe refer to this setting as held-out-dataset evaluation . It demonstrates generalization to datasets not seen during training, although the released benchmark does not independently establish a benchmark-wide leave-one-tissue-out or donor-level out-of-distribution protocol (see Table 7).\n\nPopulation benchmarks. Population samples are constructed along two orthogonal axes.\n\nA homogeneous population shares a single supervision label; a heterogeneous population intentionally mixes labels, and the model must recover the composition within the population.\n\nThree homogeneous tasks are released (cell type, 3-30 cells; tissue, 3-15; disease, 1-40) alongside two heterogeneous tasks (cell type, 16-48 cells from 2-8 types; tissue, 10-25 cells from 2-6 tissues). A heterogeneous disease task is not included in the present benchmark.\n\nConstruction uses a per-sample processing identifier on all homogeneous tasks and on the heterogeneous cell-type task to prevent cross-sample mixing; the heterogeneous tissue task intentionally relaxes this key to maximize cross-tissue diversity.\n\nAll counts in Table 6 represent released pool sizes, not final published training splits.\n\n### 4.5 Evaluation Protocol\n\nLLM-as-judge for post-training evaluation. All post-training numbers in this paper are produced by an offline evaluation judge that is implemented and run separately from the training-time reward judge (§3.7) and the training-time monitoring judge. These layers share neither a scoring scheme nor aggregation logic. Following Zheng et al. [33], the offline judge is DeepSeek-chat (DeepSeek-AI, dense) accessed through the DeepSeek cloud API. For each prediction, it emits a task-aware semantic relation label together with a continuous semantic score in [ 0, 1 ] ; this scalar is then thresholded to form a binary accuracy. For the single-cell cell-type task, we adopt a conservative operating point of τ = 0.8. Under this threshold, exact matches and strong ontology-consistent refinements are counted as correct, whereas broader lineage-adjacent or merely partially related predictions are not credited as fine-grained biological identification. For population tasks, the offline judge is applied at the per-cell level with task-specific prompt families (cell type, tissue, disease state), and per-cell scores are aggregated into sample-level summaries at a grain that depends on the reported metric (per-cellthresholded accuracy vs. per-sample mean-score correctness; see the Population evaluation paragraph below). Thresholds are task-dependent: we follow the operating points of τ = 0.75 for cell-type and tissue-type populations and the stricter τ = 0.85 for the disease-state population, consistent with the per-task figure captions in App. A.1-App. A.2.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nThe offline evaluation judge and the training-time reward judge are operationally separate judge stacks, implemented in distinct codepaths with different endpoints and prompt configurations, and served by different model families (reward judge: Qwen3-30B-A3B, Alibaba, MoE; offline evaluation judge: DeepSeek-chat, DeepSeek-AI, dense). Consequently, this separation spans multiple model-, serving-, and prompt-level axes rather than reusing the deployed judge stack from the RL phase. The intent is to reduce the risk of self-preference bias [52] in the reported metrics, not to establish formal statistical independence between the two judge stacks. 3\n\nCell Ontology-Aware Metrics. Beyond binary accuracy, we compute three additional metrics.\n\nFirst, LCAD (Lowest Common Ancestor Distance) [53, 10, 54]: a severity-of-error metric that measures the hierarchical distance between predicted and ground-truth cell types on the Cell Ontology [50, 55] is-a directed acyclic graph (DAG). Formally,\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere ct and cp represent the ground-truth and predicted cell-type labels mapped to Cell Ontology identifiers ( CL:NNNNNNN ), LCA ( ct , cp ) is their lowest common ancestor in the is-a DAG, and d ( · , · ) is the unweighted shortest-path length in the number of is-a edges.\n\nThis metric was formally introduced by Shi et al. [53] for hierarchical out-of-distribution evaluation and adapted to single-cell foundation model benchmarking by scFM-Bench [10]; the graph-distanceon-ontology family dates to Wu &amp; Palmer (1994) and Pesquita et al. [54].\n\nName resolution proceeds through a five-tier deterministic cascade before any LLM-derived signal is consulted: (i) exact string match, (ii) direct Cell Ontology lookup via the OBO Foundry release of the Cell Ontology ( cl.obo ; 2025-11-03; 3,373 CL terms), (iii) human-curated synonym mapping, (iv) rule-based string normalization, and (v) semantic-relation estimation from the offline evaluation judge (capped at 6.0; see §4.7 for the full cascade and per-dataset resolution statistics).\n\nSecond, Non-leaf accuracy : this evaluates predictions that are valid sub-types of the ground-truth label.\n\nThird, Macro-F1 and Weighted-F1 : these metrics evaluate performance across imbalanced cell-type distributions.\n\nPopulation evaluation. Population evaluation is organized by task family rather than under a single monolithic rubric. The offline evaluation pipeline covers four task families: (i) homogeneous celltype populations, (ii) heterogeneous cell-type populations, (iii) homogeneous tissue populations, and (iv) homogeneous disease-state populations. Each is scored with its own semantic-relation vocabulary (cell-type, tissue, or disease-state categories) rather than a shared joint tissue, + ,cell-type rubric.\n\nFor every family, the offline judge first parses the response into per-cell predictions, scores each cell under the task-specific rubric, and then aggregates the per-cell scores into sample-level summaries. Task-dependent thresholds ( τ = 0.75 for cell-type and tissue populations, τ = 0.85 for disease-state) are applied at the reporting grain of each table or figure rather than as a single global cutoff.\n\nBoth per-cell and per-sample statistics are retained: per-cell thresholded accuracy is reported at the cell-type grouping in App. A.2, while pass@ k (§5) is computed based on per-sample correctness (a sample is correct when its mean per-cell score clears the task threshold).\n\n3 Atraining-time monitoring judge also runs during RL to log progress metrics; it uses the same Qwen3-30B-A3B model as the reward judge and does not contribute to the policy gradient. Reward-side multi-sample aggregation (three judge samples per answer, with the mean used for the reward signal, the median used for the hard gate, and conservative tie-breaking over ontology categories) is a property of the training-time reward system and is described in App. A.4; it is not part of the offline evaluation protocol used to compute the accuracy numbers reported in the main text.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nBecause population evaluation sets are sampled per task (Table 6) rather than pooled, we report accuracy by task; the Joint (all correct) row in Table 9 acts as a locally defined summary row over the three per-task reports rather than a protocol-wide population metric.\n\nTerminology for held-out evaluation. Throughout this paper, held-out evaluation refers to evaluation on the eight held-out ScBank datasets listed above, not to a benchmark-wide tissue holdout or a donor-level closure.\n\nThe cell-type label space is shared between training and held-out datasets, as is standard in cell-type generalization studies.\n\nWe reserve the term zero-shot for baselines (e.g., LangCell) whose encoder was trained without any task-specific labels. In contrast, RVQ-Alpha is supervised and RL-trained, and its held-out numbers measure dataset-level generalization rather than label-space novelty.\n\nSplit and leakage control. We audit data separation at three levels (Table 7).\n\nRVQ representation.\n\nAll nine held-out dataset IDs (the eight evaluation datasets plus D097) are absent from the RVQ pretraining set; after fitting, evaluation cells are encoded through the frozen tokenizer without updating RVQ parameters.\n\nSupervised training.\n\nTraining, validation, and test partitions are produced by independent construction procedures, and population samples are grouped by a per-sample processing identifier on all homogeneous tasks and on the heterogeneous cell-type task.\n\nEvaluation scope.\n\nWe claim held-out-dataset evaluation and a clean RVQ representation boundary, but not strict donorlevel separation across the whole benchmark, a leave-one-tissue-out protocol, or a fully verified donor-level out-of-distribution benchmark: the released benchmark does not independently establish those stronger properties.\n\nTable 7: Split and leakage control. Nine held-out dataset IDs (eight evaluation files plus D097) define the representation-boundary scope; all main-text evaluation uses the eight-file subset only.\n\n| Level                | Mechanism                                                                                                                           | Verified                    | Scope limitation                                              |\n|----------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RVQ represen- tation | Held-out IDs excluded from the RVQ pretraining set; frozen tokenizer at evaluation time.                                            | ID-level membership check.  | Upstream donor closure of RVQ training data is not verified.  |\n| Supervised training  | Independent construction procedures for train/- val/test partitions; per- sample processing iden- tifier groups population samples. | Dataset-level separa- tion. | No unified donor-level split definition across all tasks.     |\n| Evaluation pool      | Eight held-out datasets (11,914 samples).                                                                                           | Dataset-level holdout.      | Does not establish leave- one-tissue-out or donor- level OOD. |\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nReproducibility. We release the benchmark-generation code, the sampled evaluation sets, and the reference configurations.\n\nTask-level pools can be regenerated from this code, although a self-contained benchmark distribution (with frozen donor lists and split definitions) is not included in this release and is deferred to future work.\n\n### 4.6 Baselines\n\nRVQ-Alpha emits free-form natural-language cell-type labels end-to-end, graded by an LLM judge (§4.5). The primary numerical comparison is established through a two-layer rule. Protocol compatibility requires that a candidate produce a categorical label through its native, published inference protocol so that the same LLM judge can grade it without constructing a new benchmark bridge. Capacity fairness in the primary comparison additionally restricts that set to systems whose parameter scale is close enough to our 4B backbone for an accuracy gap to be interpretable as a method gap rather than a capacity gap. Three baselines satisfy both layers:\n\n1. LangCell [24]: a Geneformer + PubMedBERT dual-encoder that performs zero-shot cell-type annotation via nearest-prototype scoring over cell-type names.\n2. Cell-o1 [30]: a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct model trained with SFT and GRPO that emits natural-language labels with reasoning chains.\n3. C2S-Scale-Gemma2-2B [20]: a Gemma-2-2B model fine-tuned on cell sentences that emits a label string under the same text-generation protocol.\n\nOther candidates anticipated by the single-cell community fall outside the primary comparison for diverse reasons rather than a single criterion. scGPT [5] and Geneformer [6] require an external protocol bridge before our judge can grade them; CellFM [7] requires task-specific fine-tuning for cell-type annotation; and C2S-Scale-Gemma2-27B [20] is protocol-compatible but, at 6.8 × the size of our backbone, falls outside the capacity-fairness layer and is therefore reported separately in §5.2 as a capacity-scaled reference rather than as a primary comparison point. App. A.6 documents the per-system rationale.\n\n### 4.7 LCAD Computation\n\nComputing LCAD (Eq. 24) requires mapping free-text predictions to Cell Ontology identifiers. We use the deterministic five-tier cascade summarized in §4.5; the only LLM-derived signal enters at Tier 4 and is bounded by a static lookup table whose maximum value is 6.0, so any reported LCAD &gt; 6 originates from the ontology graph rather than the fallback. Across the eight evaluation datasets, the ontology graph (Tiers 1-3) resolves 49.6% of LCAD computations, with per-dataset coverage ranging from 22.6% on the lowest-coverage corpus to 84.1% on the highest, and leaves no unresolved predictions. The per-dataset breakdown and the full cascade specification appear in App. A.7.\n\n## 5 Results\n\n### 5.1 Single-Cell Level Tasks\n\nTable 8 reports cell type annotation accuracy across training stages. Feature-Injection SFT establishes a strong baseline, and RLVR training yields consistent improvements across all categories.\n\nOOD generalization. The 12.1 percentage point improvement on OOD immune cells (70.7% → 82.8%, Table 8) demonstrates that RLVR training enhances out-of-distribution generalization. We attribute this to the fact verification reward, which forces the model to ground its predictions in actual expression patterns rather than memorizing RVQ-to-label mappings.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 8: Single-cell annotation accuracy (%) across training stages (Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507 base, pass@8). OOD = out-of-distribution tissues/cell types not seen during training. Both Post-SFT and Post-RLVR use the same pass@8 protocol, so the ∆ column isolates the contribution of RLVR.\n\n| Category                        |   Post-SFT |   Post-RLVR |     ∆ |\n|---------------------------------|------------|-------------|-------|\n| In-distribution (common)        |       85.3 |        89.1 |  +3.8 |\n| OODimmune datasets              |       70.7 |        82.8 | +12.1 |\n| Rare cell types (neuroblastoma) |       36.1 |        65.0 | +28.9 |\n| Average across 8 tissues        |       72.4 |        83.6 | +11.2 |\n\nControlled comparison and per-dataset losses. Aggregate accuracy obscures per-dataset variance. Across the eight evaluation datasets in Figure 5, RVQ-Alpha achieves the highest accuracy on four (A013, D095, D096, D102) but underperforms on the other four: Cell-o1 outperforms RVQ-Alpha on D094 (37.4%), and C2S-Gemma2-2B leads on D099, D101, and D103 (with the accuracy on D103 reaching 96.0%, outperforming RVQ-Alpha by a wide margin). We note a potential issue of training-data overlap for C2S-Gemma2-2B on D103, as the training corpus of this model is not fully documented [20] (see §7). In terms of LCAD (Figure 6), C2S-Gemma2-2B is closer to the ground truth on D096, D099, D102, and D103 (where its LCAD is 0.39). We report these negative results transparently to avoid selection bias, and we discuss the corresponding limitations in §7.\n\nRare cell type recognition. On rare neuroblastoma samples, accuracy nearly doubles from 36.1% to 65.0% (+28.9 pp, Table 8). LCAD analysis (Eq. 24) confirms that even incorrect predictions after RLVR are biologically closer to the ground truth: on the neuroblastoma subset, the mean LCAD drops from 5.2 (post-SFT) to 2.8 (post-RLVR), indicating that the model has learned meaningful hierarchical structure from the Cell Ontology.\n\n### 5.2 Cross-System Comparison and Methodological Scope\n\nPrimary comparison set. Figures 5-6 compare RVQ-Alpha against the three baselines selected by the two-stage criteria of §4.6 on the full 8-dataset OOD split: LangCell [24], Cell-o1 [30], and C2S-Gemma22B [20]. Each model generates free-form natural-language cell-type labels end-to-end (satisfying the protocol-compatibility criterion) and operates at a parameter scale comparable to our 4B backbone, ensuring a fair method-level comparison (satisfying the capacity-fairness criterion). RVQ-Alpha outperforms baselines on four of the eight datasets but underperforms on the remaining four (Table 16, App. A.8); we report all per-dataset numbers transparently.\n\nCellTok: the closest contemporary system. CellTok [31] is the most relevant missing comparison: both RVQ-Alpha and CellTok employ a design combining a discrete tokenizer and a Qwen-family LLM, and both operate end-to-end on cell-type labels. We do not include a numerical CellTok comparison because the official CellTok model checkpoints are unavailable at the time of writing. The CellTok preprint was posted in October 2025; the project repository 4 indicates that the model checkpoints and datasets are forthcoming. Three architectural differences between RVQ-Alpha and CellTok are documented in §3.2 (i.e., residual quantization across eight codebooks versus a single shared codebook, 10 versus 34 tokens per cell, and RLVR with fact verification versus SFT only); these distinctions do not depend on running CellTok. We will incorporate a direct comparison with CellTok once the weights become publicly available.\n\n[4 https://github.com/sunnsset/CellTok (accessed April 2026).](https://github.com/sunnsset/CellTok)\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nFigure 5: Per-dataset cell type accuracy across models. Bar-chart comparison of RVQ-Alpha (built on a Qwen3-4B backbone with the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer and GSPO reinforcement learning) against LangCell, Cell-o1, and C2S-Gemma2-2B on 8 evaluation datasets. Sampling budget: RVQ-Alpha uses pass@8 (8 stochastic decodings, best-of-8 selected by LLM judge); baselines use pass@1 (single-sample predictions from released checkpoints). This asymmetry favors RVQ-Alpha; see §5.2 for discussion. RVQ-Alpha wins on A013 (49.4%), D095 (80.9%), D096 (54.4%), and D102 (47.8%); Cell-o1 wins on D094 (37.4%); C2S-Gemma2-2B wins on D099 (64.3%), D101 (34.7%), and D103 (96.0%; see §7 for a training-data overlap caveat). LangCell is the weakest model on every dataset, peaking at 27.2% on D096. Baseline selection criteria are described in §4.6.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a grouped bar chart comparing model accuracy across datasets.\n\nVisible content:\n- Single panel bar plot.\n- X-axis label: `Datasets`.\n- Y-axis label: `Accuracy (%)`.\n- Dataset labels on x-axis: `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D099`, `D101`, `D102`, `D103`.\n- Legend at top with four model names:\n  - `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC-8_CS-32-GSPO`\n  - `LangCell`\n  - `Cell-O1`\n  - `C2S-Gemma2-2B`\n- Bars are color-coded by model.\n- Red star markers appear above the highest-performing bar for each dataset.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No biological objects, microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecular structures, or experimental specimens are visually shown.\n- The figure appears to summarize biological or cell-related benchmark results only through dataset/model names, but the plot itself contains no visible biological source imagery.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No workflow diagram, model architecture, embeddings, attention maps, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n- The only model-related information is comparative performance by named models.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC-8_CS-32-GSPO` is highest on several datasets, including `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D101`, and `D102`.\n- `C2S-Gemma2-2B` is highest on `D099` and `D103`.\n- Approximate highest values shown include about `85.0` for `D095` with Qwen, `64.3` for `D099` with C2S-Gemma2-2B, and `96.8` for `D103` with C2S-Gemma2-2B.\n- Accuracy varies substantially across datasets and models, with some models near zero or low single digits on certain datasets.\n\nBaselines outside the primary comparison set. The remaining baseline candidates fail to meet at least one of the two criteria in §4.6 for distinct reasons; we discuss them separately. Protocol mismatch. scGPT [5] and Geneformer [6] produce cell embeddings rather than labels; evaluating them under our protocol requires attaching a nearest-prototype classification head with an external text encoder, which introduces confounding factors absent from the native-protocol baselines. We include LangCell, which natively pairs a Geneformer cell encoder with a PubMedBERT text encoder, as the representative of this encoder-only family. Task adaptation. CellFM [7] requires fine-tuning its 80M variant on target labels for cell-type annotation. Capacity mismatch. C2S-Scale-Gemma2-27B [20] satisfies the protocol criterion, as it produces labels under the same end-to-end protocol as C2S-Gemma2-2B. However, with 27B parameters, it is 6.8 × larger than the 4.0B backbone of RVQ-Alpha, placing it outside the capacity-fairness criterion of the primary comparison. We already evaluate the parameter-matched counterpart, C2S-Gemma2-2B. For reference, C2S-Scale-Gemma2-27B achieves 95.43% accuracy on multi-tissue immune cell-type annotation [20]; this result should be interpreted in the context of the parameter difference.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nFigure 6: Per-dataset Lowest Common Ancestor Distance (LCAD; Eq. 24) across models. Lower is better: LCAD measures the unweighted edge distance between predicted and ground-truth cell types along is-a relations in the Cell Ontology DAG [53, 10]. Sampling budgets are identical to Figure 5: RVQ-Alpha at pass@8, baselines at pass@1. RVQ-Alpha achieves the lowest mean LCAD on A013 (2.40) and D095 (2.22); C2S-Gemma2-2B leads on four datasets (D096, D099, D102, and notably D103 with LCAD = 0.39). LangCell's bar heights span 2.79-7.53 across datasets, consistent with the cross-lineage nature of its errors. See Table 15 for the per-dataset ontology-vs-fallback resolution breakdown.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a grouped bar chart comparing `LCAD Mean` across datasets labeled `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D099`, `D101`, `D102`, and `D103`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Datasets`\n- Y-axis: `LCAD Mean`\n- Legend/model labels:\n  - `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC=8_CS=32-GSPO`\n  - `LangCell`\n  - `Cell-O1`\n  - `C2S-Gemma2-2B`\n- Each dataset has four colored bars corresponding to the four methods/models.\n- Red star markers appear above some bars, indicating highlighted or selected values, but the figure does not define their meaning.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, model architecture diagrams, or transformation workflows are visible. The figure appears to report quantitative model performance or metric comparison across datasets.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- `LangCell` has the highest bars for several datasets, including `A013`, `D095`, `D096`, `D102`, and `D103`.\n- `Cell-O1` is highest on `D099`.\n- `C2S-Gemma2-2B` is highest on `D094` and `D101`.\n- The chart compares model-level `LCAD Mean` values rather than showing raw biological imagery.\n\n### 5.3 Population-Level Tasks\n\nTable 9 summarizes population-level task accuracy across training stages.\n\nUnder the task-specific offline evaluation protocol (§4.5), the Joint (all correct) row is a local summary of the three per-task reports rather than a single cross-task metric. The 4.6 × per-task improvement on cell type classification (16.8% → 78.0%) is our strongest per-task result, demonstrating that RLVR is particularly effective for multi-cell reasoning tasks that require integrating evidence across many RVQ token sequences within a single response. Furthermore, Figure 7 shows that inference-time scaling (pass@ k ) yields monotonic accuracy gains across all three population tasks. We compute pass@ k based on per-sample correctness, where a sample is considered correct if its mean per-cell score exceeds the task threshold. Disease state prediction benefits the most (an increase of 12.8 pp from k = 1 to k = 16). The performance gap between tasks narrows at higher values of k , suggesting that inference-time scaling is particularly effective for challenging tasks where the model has learned the correct reasoning patterns but applies them inconsistently. Per-cell-type accuracy distributions across all three tasks are detailed in Appendix A.2.\n\nPer-cell reasoning. For heterogeneous populations, the reward function decomposes into per-cell reasoning judgments, which are aggregated under a tolerance threshold for missing or underdeveloped RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 9: Population-level task accuracy (%) (Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507 base; §4). Population samples contain 3-48 cells each, requiring multi-cell reasoning. Per-task rows use the task-specific offline evaluation protocol described in §4.5 (cell-type and tissue: τ = 0.75; disease-state: τ = 0.85). The Joint (all correct) row is a locally defined summary over the three per-task reports, not a protocol-wide joint tissue + cell-type metric.\n\n| Task                       |   Post-SFT |   Post-RLVR | Improvement   |\n|----------------------------|------------|-------------|---------------|\n| Cell Type Classification   |       16.8 |        78.0 | 4.6 ×         |\n| Disease State (COVID-19)   |       33.1 |        66.2 | 2.0 ×         |\n| Tissue Type Identification |       42.5 |        71.3 | 1.7 ×         |\n| Joint (all correct)        |        8.2 |        52.1 | 6.4 ×         |\n\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel line chart showing LLM judge accuracy versus number of generations `k`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Number of Generations (k)` with values `1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16`.\n- Y-axis: `LLM Judge Accuracy`, scaled from about `60%` to `95%`.\n- Legend at top with three series:\n  - `Cell Type` in blue\n  - `Disease State` in red\n  - `Tissue Type` in teal\n\nFindings shown:\n- Accuracy increases as the number of generations increases for all three categories.\n- `Cell Type` starts around `84%` at `k=1` and reaches about `88%` at `k=16`.\n- `Disease State` starts around `68%` and rises to about `81%`.\n- `Tissue Type` starts around `79%` and reaches about `90%`.\n- At `k=16`, highlighted diamond markers indicate final values, with `Tissue Type` highest, `Cell Type` slightly lower, and `Disease State` lowest.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The plotted categories refer to biological/biomedical metadata labels: cell type, disease state, and tissue type.\n- No actual biological images, cells, tissue microscopy, or molecular structures are visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize an LLM judging or classification/evaluation process over multiple generated outputs.\n- No model architecture, interface diagram, or data transformation pipeline is shown.\n\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\nFigure 7: Pass@ k accuracy across population-level tasks. LLM judge accuracy improves with the number of sampled generations k . Disease state (red) benefits most (+12.8 pp), and the inter-task gap narrows at higher k . Final ranking at k = 16: Tissue Type (90.0%) &gt; Cell Type (88.0%) &gt; Disease State (80.8%).\n\ncells (Appendix A.4). This mechanism forces the model to produce genuine multi-cell analyses rather than resorting to pattern-matching shortcuts that exploit a single salient cell.\n\n### 5.4 Ablation Studies\n\nTable 10 summarizes the contribution of each RVQ-Alpha component. We isolate one component at a time, retrain Stage 3, and re-evaluate the model on the held-out single-cell and OOD splits. The Evidence-First constraint and Fact Verification module contribute the largest gains, while the iterated reward-hacking defenses prevent degenerate output behaviors that would otherwise dominate training.\n\nEffect of Evidence-First constraint. Removing the Evidence-First requirement (allowing direct label prediction without gene evidence) reduces single-cell accuracy by 8.3 pp on average and increases RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning Notes (qualitative failure modes). (i) Without the specificity-penalty gate , the model collapses to enumerating many gene markers without committing to a cell-type call. (ii) Without the prompt-imitation detector , 12% of generations contain near-verbatim system-prompt fragments; enabling the detector reduces this to &lt; 1%. Both behaviours are eliminated by the dual hard gates A ′ and A ′′ (Appendix A.4).\n\nTable 10: Ablation of RVQ-Alpha components. Each row removes one component from the full system; ∆ is reported relative to the full reference. Single-cell Acc is the mean accuracy across the eight single-cell datasets; OOD Acc is accuracy on out-of-distribution immune datasets; Hallu. Rate is the LLM-judge-detected rate of unsupported biological claims; LCAD &gt; 5 Rate is the fraction of predictions whose LCAD distance (Eq. 24) exceeds 5 is-a edges. Two further qualitative failure modes that motivated hard gates A ′ and A ′′ are reported in the Notes below; full reward-design history is in Appendix A.4.\n\n| Configuration               | Single-cell Acc   | OODAcc   | Hallu. Rate   | LCAD > 5 Rate   |\n|-----------------------------|-------------------|----------|---------------|-----------------|\n| Full RVQ-Alpha (reference)  | 83.6%             | 82.8%    | 4.2%          | 1.00 ×          |\n| - Evidence-First constraint | - 8.3pp           | -        | 23.7%         | -               |\n| - Fact Verification         | -                 | - 6.5pp  | -             | 1.41 ×          |\n\nthe hallucination rate from 4.2% to 23.7% (Table 10, row 2). This confirms that explicit gene-level grounding is essential for the LLM-native tokenization scheme.\n\nEffect of Fact Verification. Disabling the Fact Verification module while keeping other reward components reduces OOD accuracy by 6.5pp and increases biologically implausible predictions (LCAD &gt; 5 edges; Eq. 24) by 41% (Table 10, row 3). This demonstrates that expression-grounded verification is the primary driver for generalization to unseen distributions.\n\nEffect of defense mechanisms. The marker-stuffing defense resolves a reward-hacking pattern where the model over-specifies predictions with excessive gene markers; enabling it restores answer quality while maintaining a stable reward signal (Table 10, Notes (i)). The template-regurgitation detector reduces system-prompt copying from 12% to &lt; 1% of responses (Table 10, Notes (ii)). Both defenses are implemented through the dual hard gates A ′ and A ′′ described in Appendix A.4.\n\n## 6 Analysis\n\n### 6.1 RVQ Compression Quality\n\nTable 11 compares six configurations of the RVQ tokenizer on held-out cells, parameterized by the codebook count and the codebook size, denoted throughout this section as ( Nc , C ) . The chosen configuration, ( 8, 32 ) , achieves the best score on 7 of the 9 metrics: it minimizes MSE (0.0738) and MMD(0.0298), maximizes per-cell Pearson correlation (0.704) and ACS (0.708), and yields the highest scores across all three clustering metrics (ARI 0.247, AMI 0.557, NMI 0.589). It remains competitive on the other two metrics, ranking second in codebook usage (52.31%, trailing ( 32, 32 ) at 55.18%) and third in per-gene Pearson correlation (0.116).\n\nTwo configurations illustrate the failure modes avoided by ( 8, 32 ) . Wide-and-shallow codebooks ( 16, 512 ) underutilize capacity: only 9.81% of the 8,192 entries are actively used, leaving over 90% dormant. Narrow-and-deep sequences ( 128, 8 ) , conversely, suffer uniform degradation across all reconstruction metrics (PCC c drops to 0.652, ACS drops to 0.661, MMD rises to 0.0388) without providing any clustering benefits. Thus, ( 8, 32 ) combines optimal reconstruction and clustering quality with a minimal footprint on the LLM side (only 256 added vocabulary entries and 8 tokens per cell), making it the standard configuration for the remainder of RVQ-Alpha.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 11: RVQtokenizer configuration ablation. Evaluation of six ( Nc , C ) variants on held-out cells, where Nc is the codebook count and C is the codebook size. Usage indicates the percentage of actively used codebook entries; PCCc/PCCg denote per-cell/per-gene Pearson correlation; ACS is the average cosine similarity; MMD is the maximum mean discrepancy; ARI/AMI/NMI are clustering metrics based on held-out cell-type labels. Bold text marks the best score per column; ⋆ marks the configuration adopted by RVQ-Alpha, which attains the best score on 7 of the 9 metrics.\n\n| ( N c , C )   |   Usage% |   MSE ↓ |   PCC c ↑ |   PCC g ↑ |   ACS ↑ |   MMD ↓ |   ARI ↑ |   AMI ↑ |   NMI ↑ |\n|---------------|----------|---------|-----------|-----------|---------|---------|---------|---------|---------|\n| ( 8, 32 ) ⋆   |    52.31 |  0.0738 |     0.704 |     0.116 |   0.708 |  0.0298 |   0.247 |   0.557 |   0.589 |\n| ( 8, 256 )    |    24.02 |  0.0775 |     0.690 |     0.113 |   0.692 |  0.0332 |   0.245 |   0.552 |   0.584 |\n| ( 16, 64 )    |    43.46 |  0.0771 |     0.694 |     0.115 |   0.698 |  0.0327 |   0.220 |   0.543 |   0.577 |\n| ( 16, 512 )   |     9.81 |  0.0796 |     0.697 |     0.117 |   0.702 |  0.0368 |   0.217 |   0.521 |   0.556 |\n| ( 32, 32 )    |    55.18 |  0.0759 |     0.695 |     0.118 |   0.698 |  0.0309 |   0.214 |   0.541 |   0.575 |\n| ( 128, 8 )    |    50.39 |  0.0801 |     0.652 |     0.105 |   0.661 |  0.0388 |   0.220 |   0.535 |   0.569 |\n\n### 6.2 Semantic Understanding of RVQ Tokens\n\nEvidence for the hierarchy hypothesis. Section 3.2 hypothesizes that the variance-ordered decomposition produced by residual quantization exhibits a coarse-to-fine correspondence with the hierarchical organization of cellular identity. Figure 8 supports this hypothesis: a directed-graph analysis over 100K class-balanced cells shows that earlier codebooks (CB-0-CB-2) predominantly partition cells at broader identity levels (major cell groups, representative subtypes, shared cell states), whereas later codebooks (CB-3-CB-4) refine within-lineage variation into functional programs and pathway markers. We note that this serves as evidence rather than formal proof; the decomposition allows, but does not guarantee, such an alignment, as residual codebooks are trained symmetrically.\n\nSemantic structure of predictions. To assess whether the LLM develops a biological understanding of RVQtokens rather than merely memorizing mappings, we examine the semantic confusion structure of its predictions alongside the corresponding embedding-space organization in Appendix A.1 (Figures 10 and 11). The confusion matrix shows that most off-diagonal mass concentrates between ontologically adjacent cell types rather than across unrelated lineages, confirming that RVQ tokenization preserves the local semantic structure of gene expression. The companion t-SNE visualization shows that biologically related cell types cluster together in the RVQ embedding space of the LLM. Neuronal cells form a distinct cluster separated from immune and epithelial lineages, while immune subtypes (T cells, NK, Myeloid) exhibit substantial overlap, which is consistent with their shared ontological proximity. Based on these complementary views, we hypothesize three properties regarding the underlying interaction between RVQ and reasoning: (1) attention over RVQ positions tracks biologically relevant gene modules; (2) attention shifts across codebook positions depending on the biological property the model evaluates (lineage versus functional state); and (3) codebook combinations unseen during training continue to yield biologically coherent predictions. We state these explicitly as hypotheses, leaving direct evidence from attention patterns to future work. Companion confusion matrices for tissue type and disease state, along with an alluvial diagram of misclassification flows, are provided in the same appendix (Figures 12-14).\n\n### 6.3 Reward System Dynamics\n\nThe piecewise gated reward (Eq. 19) yields stable training dynamics, with the positive path ( s + ans &gt; 0) and the negative path remaining well-separated throughout training. The two hard gates, A ′ and A ′′ , eliminate three reward-hacking modes that emerged during training (marker stuffing, template regurgitation, and multi-pattern over-specification), which are documented in Table 10.\n\nFigure 9a illustrates the joint progression of response length and accuracy during RLVR training.\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic flow diagram showing a hierarchical biological classification framework from “All Cells” through five labeled concept-block stages:\n\n- **CB-0 Major Cell Group**: Immune (03), Epithelial (30), Stromal Mesenchymal (07), Endothelial (24), Neural Glial (25), Parenchymal Other (10).\n- **CB-1 Representative Subtype**: T/NK (00), B/Plasma (30), Myeloid (03), Basal (01), Secretory (22), Absorptive (09), Fibroblast (23), Pericyte/SMC (28), Myofibroblast (15), Capillary (04), Arterial/Venous (17), Lymphatic (05), Neuron (31), Astro/Oligo (14), Microglia/Schwann (16), Tissue-specific (26), Other (07).\n- **CB-2 Shared Cell State**: Proliferating (12), Quiescent (18), Activated Inflammatory (08), Stressed (11), Differentiating Transitional (09), Senescent Damage-associated (10).\n- **CB-3 Functional Program**: Cell Cycle (03), Proteostasis Translation (03), OXPHOS Metabolism (19), Immune Inflammatory Response (18), ECM Adhesion (28), Secretory Effector Program (08).\n- **CB-4 Pathway Activity**: IFN / NF-KB (03), TGFβ / EMT (14), WNT / Notch (19), mTOR / MYC (03), Hypoxia / P53 (27).\n\nColored vertical backgrounds separate the blocks: green for major cell group, blue for subtype, yellow for shared cell state, gray for functional program, and peach for pathway activity. Curved connector lines show mappings between categories across blocks. Black/gray lines connect cell groups to representative subtypes, blue lines connect subtypes and states to functional programs, and orange lines connect functional programs to pathway activities.\n\nThe biological source objects represented are cell populations, cell subtypes, cell states, functional transcriptional/metabolic programs, and pathway activities. The figure appears to summarize a cell annotation or concept-bottleneck style taxonomy linking cell identity to shared biological states, functional programs, and signaling/pathway activity categories.\n\nFigure 8: RVQ codebook hierarchy across cell lineages. Directed graph over 100K class-balanced cells encoded by the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer, showing how successive codebook levels decompose cell identity from coarse lineage to fine-grained pathway activity. Five columns are labeled: CB-0: Major Cell Group (Immune, Epithelial, Stromal Mesenchymal, Endothelial, Neural Glial, Parenchymal Other), CB-1: Representative Subtype (17 nodes, e.g. T/NK, B/Plasma, Myeloid, Fibroblast, Capillary, Neuron), CB-2: Shared Cell State (Proliferating, Quiescent, Activated Inflammatory, Stressed, Differentiating Transitional, Senescent Damage-associated), CB-3: Functional Program (Cell Cycle, Proteostasis, OXPHOS Metabolism, Immune Inflammatory Response, ECM Adhesion, Secretory Effector), and CB-4: Pathway Activity (IFN/NFκ B, TGFβ /EMT, WNT/Notch, mTOR/MYC, Hypoxia/P53). The structure transitions from a strict hierarchy (black edges, CB-0 → CB-1) to a many-to-many combinatorial mapping (blue edges, CB-1 → CB-2 → CB-3; orange edges, CB-3 → CB-4), demonstrating that earlier codebooks capture coarse lineage identity while later codebooks encode cross-cutting cell states, functional programs, and pathway markers.\n\nResponse length initially rises during exploration, peaks at ∼ 9,500 tokens around step 250, and then contracts to ∼ 7,000 tokens as accuracy continues to improve. This demonstrates an RL-induced compression of the reasoning chains.\n\nTo validate that the dimensions of reasoning quality improve concurrently with task accuracy, Figure 9b plots all six dimensions against the accuracy of the LLM judge over the training steps. All dimensions exhibit strong positive correlations ( r = 0.60-0.92), confirming that the RLVR reward system drives genuine improvements in reasoning quality alongside task performance.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n####### \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single time-series line plot showing training dynamics over `Training Step` on the x-axis. The left y-axis is labeled `Response Length / Max (tokens)` in teal, and the right y-axis is labeled `LLM Judge Accuracy (mean@1)` in red.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Teal curve: `Response Length (smoothed, w=25)` with faint noisy raw trace behind it.\n- Red curve: `LLM Judge Accuracy (smoothed, w=5)` with faint raw trace behind it.\n- Three shaded training phases:\n  - Blue region labeled `Initial`\n  - Orange region labeled `Converging`\n  - Green region labeled `Stable`\n- The response length rises from roughly 2,000 tokens to peaks near 9,000-10,000 tokens during convergence, then declines and stabilizes around 6,500-7,000 tokens.\n- The LLM judge accuracy starts near 0.15-0.2, fluctuates during early/converging phases, then increases and stabilizes around 0.7-0.8.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: smoothed training metrics are plotted over raw metric traces; the figure compares response length behavior with LLM judge accuracy during model training.\n\nFinding shown: accuracy improves and stabilizes while response length eventually decreases from an overlong peak and settles into a stable range.\n\n(a) Response length (teal) initially rises during exploration, peaks at ∼ 9,500 tokens around step 250, and then contracts to ∼ 7,000 tokens as accuracy (red) continues to improve. Three training phases are labeled: Initial, Converging, and Stable.\n\n####### \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with six line/scatter subplots arranged in a 2 x 3 grid.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: “Reasoning Quality Weighted Score (r=0.91)”\n- Top middle: “Independence (r=0.87)”\n- Top right: “Robustness (r=0.60)”\n- Bottom left: “Causal (r=0.84)”\n- Bottom middle: “Granularity (r=0.92)”\n- Bottom right: “Consistency (r=0.85)”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Each subplot uses “Training Step” on the x-axis, spanning roughly 0 to 800.\n- Left y-axis is labeled “Accuracy” in blue.\n- Right y-axis is labeled “Something Capacity” or similar in red; the exact wording is hard to read.\n- Blue scatter points and a smoothed blue trend line represent accuracy.\n- Red scatter points and a smoothed red trend line represent the second metric.\n- Faint dashed trend/reference lines appear in several panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. This is not a biological microscopy, anatomical, molecular, or specimen image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize model training dynamics over training steps.\n- Metrics are plotted as scatter observations with smoothed fitted curves.\n- Correlation coefficients are reported in each panel title.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Accuracy generally increases with training steps across all panels.\n- The red metric also generally increases over training, though with different trajectories by metric.\n- Strong positive correlations are shown for reasoning quality weighted score, independence, causal, granularity, and consistency.\n- Robustness shows a weaker positive relationship, with r=0.60.\n\n(b) Each panel shows LLM judge accuracy (blue) and a reasoning quality dimension score (red) over training steps, with Pearson correlations: Granularity ( r = 0.92), Weighted Score ( r = 0.91), Consistency ( r = 0.88), Independence ( r = 0.87), Causal ( r = 0.84), Robustness ( r = 0.60).\n\nFigure 9: Reward system dynamics for cell-type classification. (a) Training dynamics: response length vs. accuracy. (b) Concurrent improvement of task accuracy and reasoning quality dimensions.\n\nAnalogous plots for tissue type and disease state classification appear in Supplementary Figures 18, 19, 20, and 21, revealing a systematic gradient: reasoning-quality correlations weaken from cell type ( r ∈ [ 0.60, 0.92 ] ) through tissue type ( r ∈ [ 0.10, 0.74 ] ) to disease state ( r ∈ [ -0.13, 0.70 ] ), with Robustness turning negative for disease state.\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n## 7 Limitations\n\nRVQ-Alpha presents several limitations that provide directions for future work:\n\n###### RVQ information loss.\n\nCompressing ∼ 20,000 gene dimensions into 8 discrete tokens inherently discards fine-grained expression information. Although this representation is sufficient for cell type classification, the loss of information may degrade performance on tasks that require precise quantitative expression values (e.g., differential expression fold-change prediction).\n\n###### Evaluation scope.\n\nWe report results on a held-out pool of eight ScBank datasets (12 tissues, five diseases, 106 cell types; §4.4). This dataset-level held-out protocol is less rigorous than benchmark-wide leave-one-tissue-out or donor-level out-of-distribution (OOD) evaluation. Furthermore, generalization to other dataset compositions, non-human organisms, and rare pathological states remains to be validated.\n\n###### Computational cost.\n\nEach RLVR step issues multiple LLM-as-judge queries per generated response (three samples per response in our setup, adding approximately one second of latency per query). Consequently, the judge dominates the wall-clock time of large-scale RLVR training. Future work could address this by distilling the judge into a lightweight classifier.\n\n###### Gene name dependency.\n\nDuring training, Feature-Injection SFT relies on the topK gene names. At inference time, without gene injection, the model must rely entirely on learned RVQ semantics; this dependence can degrade performance for very rare cell types that have limited training coverage.\n\n###### Population scale.\n\nDue to context length constraints, current population-level tasks are limited to 3-48 cells per sample. Scaling to atlas-level populations (thousands of cells) requires efficient attention mechanisms or hierarchical summarization techniques.\n\n###### Disease state discrimination.\n\nFor disease state classification, the semantic confusion matrix (Figure 14) reveals that four out of five disease classes collapse into the healthy class. This indicates that the current reward signal lacks the contrast necessary to train disease discrimination under severe class imbalance.\n\n###### Baseline comparison scope.\n\nThe cross-system comparison (Figure 5; Table 16, App. A.8) covers the three primary baselines selected by the two-stage criteria of §4.6 (protocol compatibility and capacity fairness). We omit other candidates from the primary comparison for various reasons: scGPT, Geneformer, and CellFM fail the protocolcompatibility criterion (because they yield encoder-only outputs or require task adaptation), whereas C2S-Scale-27B is protocol-compatible but exceeds the capacity limit of our 4B backbone. We discuss both groups in §5.2 and App. A.6 without numerical evaluation. Finally, CellTok [31] cannot be compared until its official weights are released.\n\n###### Baseline training-data overlap.\n\nThe training corpus of C2S-Gemma2-2B is not fully documented [20]. Thus, we cannot verify whether any of the eight evaluation datasets appeared in its training data. This is particularly concerning for dataset D103, where C2S-Gemma2-2B achieves 96.0% accuracy at pass@8. We advise readers to consider this caveat when interpreting the per-dataset comparisons in Table 16 (App. A.8).\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n###### Single base model.\n\nWe report all results using a single base model (Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507, 4.0 B parameters; §4). We do not report variance estimates across random initializations; characterizing seed-level variance remains a direction for future work.\n\n## 8 Conclusion\n\nWe presented RVQ-Alpha, an end-to-end framework that bridges single-cell transcriptomics and large language models through three key components: RVQ discrete tokenization, Evidence-First FeatureInjection SFT, and RLVR with fact verification. By converting continuous gene expression profiles into discrete tokens and embedding them directly into the LLM vocabulary, RVQ-Alpha achieves bidirectional unification, enabling both the interpretation and generation of cell representations within a single model.\n\nEmpirical results demonstrate that RLVR with domain-specific verifiable rewards yields a 4.6 × performance improvement on population classification, a 28.9 pp increase in accuracy for rare cell types, and a 12.1 pp gain in out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization (Tables 8 and 9). The fact verification module is critical for grounding model outputs in actual expression data, thereby reducing hallucinations and enhancing generalization (Table 10).\n\nRVQ-Alpha establishes a foundation for AI Virtual Cells, providing a scalable, interpretable, and generative substrate for multi-modal biological intelligence. Future work will extend this framework to support cross-species generalization, atlas-scale populations, and the integration of additional modalities (e.g., spatial transcriptomics and chromatin accessibility).\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n### References\n\n- [1] Charlotte Bunne, Yusuf Roohani, Yanay Rosen, et al. How to build the virtual cell with artificial intelligence: Priorities and opportunities. Cell , 2024.\n- [2] Dragomirka Jovic, Xue Liang, Hua Zeng, Lin Lin, Fengping Xu, and Yonglun Luo. Single-cell RNA sequencing technologies and applications: A brief overview. Clinical and Translational Medicine , 12(3):e694, 2022.\n- [3] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. Attention is all you need. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) , volume 30, 2017.\n- [4] Jason Wei, Xuezhi Wang, Dale Schuurmans, et al. Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning in large language models. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) , volume 35, 2022.\n- [5] Haotian Cui, Chloe Wang, Hassaan Maan, Kuan Pang, Fengning Luo, Nan Duan, and Bo Wang. scGPT: toward building a foundation model for single-cell multi-omics using generative AI. Nature Methods , 21:1470-1480, 2024.\n- [6] Christina V. Theodoris, Ling Xiao, Anant Chopra, et al. Transfer learning enables predictions in network biology. Nature , 618:616-624, 2023.\n- [7] Yuansong Zeng, Jiancong Xie, Ningyuan Shangguan, et a