��\n- Right y-axis appears in red and represents the corresponding quality metric for each panel\n- Blue scatter points and blue smoothed curves show accuracy trends\n- Red scatter points and red smoothed curves show metric trends\n- A faint red dashed horizontal reference line is visible in several panels\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. This is not a biological microscopy, anatomy, molecular, or organism image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to compare model training progression against evaluation metrics.\n- Smoothed curves are overlaid on raw scatter observations.\n- Correlation coefficients are reported in panel titles.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- Accuracy generally increases during early training and then plateaus or slightly declines in later steps.\n- Reasoning Quality Weighted Score, Independence, Causal, Granularity, and Consistency show strong positive correlations with accuracy, with reported r values around +0.71 to +0.74.\n- Robustness shows little correlation with accuracy, reported as r=+0.10.\n- Several red metric curves rise sharply around mid-training steps, especially in the reasoning quality, independence, causal, granularity, and consistency panels.\n\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\nFigure 20: Tissue type - accuracy vs. reasoning quality. Companion to Figure 9b for homogeneous tissue-type classification (the converged GSPO tissue policy, ∼ 750 training steps). LLM judge accuracy (blue, left axis, range 0.450-0.650) rises from ∼ 0.495 to a plateau near ∼ 0.59-0.60 from step ∼ 300, with a slight terminal downturn. Per-dimension Pearson correlations: Granularity ( r = + 0.74), Reasoning Quality Weighted ( r = + 0.73), Independence ( r = + 0.73), Causal ( r = + 0.72), Consistency ( r = + 0.71), and Robustness ( r = + 0.10). Most reasoning curves (red, right axis) rise sharply between steps ∼ 100300 and then plateau, mirroring the accuracy trajectory. All correlations are noticeably weaker than the cell-type range [ 0.60, 0.92 ] ; Robustness is essentially flat ( r = + 0.10), foreshadowing its collapse to negative values on disease state (Figure 21).\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n####### \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot arranged as a 2 x 3 grid. Each panel shows scatter points with smoothed trend lines over training progression.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Top left: “Reasoning Quality Weighted Score (r=0.70)”\n- Top middle: “Independence (r=0.38)”\n- Top right: “Robustness (r=-0.13)”\n- Bottom left: “Causal (r=0.36)”\n- Bottom middle: “Granularity (r=0.07)”\n- Bottom right: “Consistency (r=0.46)”\n\nAxes and encodings:\n- X-axis in all panels: “Training Step”\n- Left Y-axis: “Accuracy,” shown in blue\n- Right Y-axis: panel-specific metric, shown in red\n- Blue scatter points and solid blue smoothed lines represent accuracy trends\n- Red scatter points and dashed red smoothed lines represent the corresponding evaluation metric trend\n- Each panel includes light gridlines and dual Y-axes\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. The figure does not show cells, tissues, molecules, organisms, microscopy, sequencing data, or other biological objects.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize model training/evaluation behavior across training steps.\n- No architecture diagram, model interface, input/output schema, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Accuracy generally increases across training steps in all panels, with an early dip around roughly 200-250 training steps.\n- Reasoning Quality Weighted Score shows a positive association with accuracy, reported as r=0.70.\n- Consistency also shows a positive association, r=0.46.\n- Independence and Causal show weaker positive associations, r=0.38 and r=0.36.\n- Robustness shows a slight negative association, r=-0.13.\n- Granularity shows near-zero association, r=0.07.\n\n\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\n\nFigure 21: Disease state - accuracy vs. reasoning quality. Companion to Figure 9b for homogeneous disease-state classification (the converged GSPO disease-state policy, ∼ 600 training steps). LLM judge accuracy (blue, left axis, range 0.28-0.42) follows a U-shaped recovery: starts ∼ 0.33, dips to ∼ 0.31 around steps 200-250, then rises to ∼ 0.40 by step 600. Per-dimension Pearson correlations: Reasoning Quality Weighted ( r = + 0.70), Consistency ( r = + 0.46), Independence ( r = + 0.38), Causal ( r = + 0.36), Granularity ( r = + 0.07), and Robustness ( r = -0.13). Two dimensions collapse on this hardest task: Robustness turns negative ( r = -0.13; right-axis range -0.40 to 0.00, monotonic decline then partial recovery), the only negative correlation among the 18 (task, dimension) pairs. Granularity drops to near zero ( r = + 0.07; right-axis range -0.35 to 0.00), down from r = + 0.92 on cell type. This forms the cross-task correlation gradient: Cell Type (strong, r = 0.60-0.92) &gt; Tissue Type (moderate, r = 0.10-0.74) &gt; Disease State (weak, r = -0.13 to + 0.70), providing the strongest evidence that the reward signal loses traction on hard tasks and motivating task-specific reward tuning as future work.\n\n\nCORRECTIONS REQUIRED FOR PRIOR OUTPUTS:\n- dense coverage missing=['dense::june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121::0001', 'dense::june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121::0002', 'dense::june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121::0003', 'dense::june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121::0004', 'dense::june_u