T-CYB MT-CO2 MT-CO1 MT-ND3 MT-ND4L ...\n\nCell 10: RPS27 CD74 MALAT1 RPL41 RPLP1 EEF1A1 RPS12 RPL18A RPL39 MT-ND3 RPS8 ...\n\nCell 11: MALAT1 MT-CO2 MT-ND3 MT-ATP6 B2M MT-CYB MT-CO1 MT-CO3 MT-ND1 SAT1 ...\n\nCell type-conditioned sample generation (multi-cell) Prompt: Produce a list of 254 gene names in order of descending expression for 8 different Homo sapiens cells with the following cell types: Cell 1: naive thymus-derived CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell. Cell 2: CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell. Cell 3: naive thymus-derived CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell. Cell 4: group 3 innate lymphoid cell. Cell 5: memory B cell. Cell 6: effector memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell. Cell 7: memory B cell. Cell 8: naive thymus-derived CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell. Cell sentences: Response: Cell 1: EEF1A1 RPS12 RPL13 RPLP1 RPS27A RPL41 RPL30 RPL34 RPL32 RPL10 RPS23 ... Cell 2: MALAT1 EEF1A1 MTRNR2L8 RPLP1 TPT1 RPS12 RPL41 RPL13 RPL10 FOS RPS19 ... Cell 3: EEF1A1 TPT1 RPL41 RPLP1 RPL10 MALAT1 RPS8 RPS12 RPL32 RPL13 RPS27 ... Cell 4: EEF1A1 MTRNR2L8 TPT1 B2M RPL10 RPLP1 RPL41 RPS27A RPL30 RPL19 RPL34 ... Cell 5: MALAT1 EEF1A1 JUN RPL41 RPLP1 RPS18 RPS8 RPS27 RPS12 RPL13 RPL10 MT-CO1 ... Cell 6: EEF1A1 RPLP1 TPT1 MALAT1 RPL13 RPL41 RPS12 RPS27 RPL10 RPS18 RPS8 ... Cell 7: RPLP1 EEF1A1 MALAT1 CD74 RPL41 RPS12 RPL13 RPS18 TMSB4X RPS27 RPS14 ... Cell 8: MALAT1 EEF1A1 RPS12 RPL13 RPS27 TPT1 RPL41 RPLP1 RPL10 RPS27A RPL32 ...\n\nFigure 12: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale pretraining generation tasks: cell type and tissue conditional generation for single cells and multi-cell samples.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\n#### Dataset interpretation\n\nPrompt: Here is a sequence of the highest expressed 338 genes from 6 different Homo sapiens cells, ordered by descending expression. Using this, your task is to create an abstract summary which summarizes the biological insights contained in these cells.\n\nCell 1: RPLP1 RPS12 EEF1A1 MALAT1 RPL41 RPS15A RPL39 RPS27 TPT1 RPS27A RPL10 ...\n\nCell 2: MALAT1 CD74 EEF1A1 RPLP1 MTRNR2L8 RPL41 FTH1 RPL30 RPS27 JUND RPL10 ...\n\nCell 3: JUN MALAT1 KLF2 EEF1A1 RPLP1 JUND RPS27 RPS12 RPL41 CD74 B2M RPL13 TPT1 ...\n\nCell 4: EEF1A1 RPLP1 RPS12 MT-CO1 RPL41 RPL10 RPS8 MALAT1 RPL13 TPT1 RPS23 RPL28 ...\n\nCell 5: RPLP1 EEF1A1 RPL10 RPS12 RPL41 RPL30 MALAT1 RPS27A TPT1 RPS27 RPL32 ...\n\nCell 6: MALAT1 RPLP1 EEF1A1 RPL41 RPS27 RPS28 RPL10 RPS14 RPL13 MTRNR2L8 ...\n\nAbstract summary:\n\nResponse: A study using single-cell RNA and VDJ sequencing of immune cells from 16 human tissues developed a machine learning tool, CellTypist, for precise cell type annotation. This approach revealed tissuespecific features and clonal architecture of T and B cells, providing insight into the distribution of immune cell types across tissues. The multitissue approach establishes a foundation for identifying highly resolved immune cell types and understanding their tissue-specific characteristics.\n\n#### Abstract-conditioned sample generation\n\nPrompt: Interpret the abstract summary below from a single-cell dataset. Create a list of 192 genes in order of decreasing expression for 11 distinct Homo sapiens cells that you would expect based on the abstract. Abstract summary:\n\nThe study used single-cell RNA sequencing and VDJ sequencing to analyze the immune compartment in 16 human tissues from 12 donors, identifying 360,000 cells. They developed a machine learning tool, CellTypist, for accurate cell type annotation, revealing tissue-specific immune cell features and clonal architecture of T and B cells. This multitissue approach provides a foundation for identifying detailed immune cell types using a common reference dataset and tissue-integrated analysis.\n\nCell sentences:\n\nResponse: Cell 1: MALAT1 HSPA1A DNAJB1 HSP90AA1 H3-3B EEF1A1 JUND KLF2 UBC CD74 ... Cell 2: MALAT1 MT-CO1 ZFP36 EEF1A1 B2M RPL10 JUND HLA-B RPS12 HLA-A BTG1 RPS27 ... Cell 3: B2M CCL3 ZFP36 MALAT1 HLA-B HSPA5 HLA-C GNLY HLA-A EEF1A1 RPL41 MT-CO1 ... Cell 4: MALAT1 EEF1A1 RPS27 B2M RPL41 HLA-B RPL10 HLA-A HLA-C MT-CO1 RPL13 RPLP1 ... Cell 5: CD74 RPS27 RPL13 RPLP1 RPL41 EEF1A1 RPS12 MALAT1 BTG1 RPS27A RPS28 ... Cell 6: KLF6 EEF1A1 RPS12 RPL28 RPL10 RPL41 RPS23 MALAT1 RPS8 B2M RPLP1 RPL32 ... Cell 7: MALAT1 EEF1A1 RPS12 RPL13 TPT1 RPL11 RPL41 RPLP1 RPS27 RPL30 RPS8 RPS23 ... Cell 8: MALAT1 RPLP1 EEF1A1 JUND TMSB4X TPT1 MT-CO1 RPS12 HLA-B BTG1 MT-CO2 ... Cell 9: ACTB TMSB4X TMSB10 RPL13 RPS8 RPS23 RPL41 RPL10 RPS13 RPLP1 RPS6 FTH1 ... Cell 10: MALAT1 MT-CO1 RPS12 RPS27 TPT1 RPL11 RPL41 EEF1A1 RPS27A RPL32 RPL37 ... Cell 11: MALAT1 EEF1A1 RPLP1 RPL41 RPS27 RPS12 RPS8 RPL13 RPL30 RPS15A RPL10 ...\n\nFigure 13: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale pretraining dataset interpretation tasks: dataset interpretation from multi-cell samples and abstract-conditioned sample generation.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 14: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale downstream natural language tasks: cluster captioning and question answering.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel scientific figure showing examples of a language-model style interface for single-cell biology tasks.\n\nTop panel label: “Cluster captioning”\n- Shows a prompt asking to “Describe this single-cell cluster.”\n- Input consists of 5 cells with genes listed in descending order of expression.\n- Visible gene examples include MALAT1, DPP10, RBFOX1, SNHG14, ZNF385D, LRP1B, MEG3, NRXN1, PCDH9, NRG3, DLG2.\n- The response describes a cerebral cortex neuron class with adhesion and signaling programs, mentioning DSCAM, NXPH2, FGFR1, FGF ligands, HTR2C, GRM3/GRM4/GRM8, and GHR.\n- Biological source object: single-cell gene expression profiles from a neuronal cluster.\n- Transformation/interface: raw per-cell ranked gene lists are converted into a natural-language cluster caption.\n\nBottom panel label: “Question answering”\n- Shows a prompt about endothelial cells profiled by single-cell RNA sequencing in diseased tissue.\n- Input lists 10 cells with cell type, tissue, disease status, and ranked gene expression.\n- Visible cell types include fibroblast, endothelial cell, glial cell, pericyte, and myofibroblast cell.\n- Tissues include ileal epithelium, lamina propria of small intestine, small intestine, and ileum.\n- Disease labels include Crohn disease and normal.\n- Visible genes include MALAT1, MT-ATP6, MT-ND3, MT-CO3, MT-CO1, B2M, MT-CO2, MT-CYB, ADAMDEC1, IGFBP7, RGS5, ACTB, MYL9, TPM2, CFD, TMSB4X.\n- The question asks what can be inferred from raw endothelial cell sentences about potential markers of vascular inflammation.\n- The response identifies CD74, IFI27, and HLA-DRA as possible vascular inflammation markers.\n- Biological source object: single-cell RNA-seq-derived cell sentences from intestinal/Crohn disease context.\n- Transformation/interface: structured cell metadata and gene-expression text are used for question answering.\n- Finding shown: the model suggests immune/inflammation-associated endothelial markers, specifically CD74, IFI27, and HLA-DRA.\n\n#### Perturbation effect prediction - PBMC\n\nPrompt: Analyze how IL-6 affects the expression hierarchy of CD4 T cells, presented as an ordered list of genes by decreasing expression in 'Source:', under chronic stimulation.\n\nSource: MALAT1 RPS12 RPS13 RPS6 RPL3 RPS4X B2M FTL RPLP0 RPS5 RPL39 RPS26 ACTB MT-CO3 HLA-A MT-ATP6 HLA-C TRBV9 TMSB10 S100A6 IFITM1 HLA-E PDE3B MT-CYB KLRK1 TXN ...\n\nResponse: MALAT1 RPS12 RPS13 RPS6 B2M RPLP0 RPL39 RPS4X RPL3 FTL MTRNR2L12 ACTB MT-ATP6 RPS5 MT-CYB HLA-C HLA-A MT-CO3 IFITM1 IL32 TMSB10 HLA-E SELL S100A6 ...\n\n#### Perturbation effect prediction - L1000\n\nPrompt: Based on the conditions specified below, analyze the response of the following bulk RNA-seq cell line, represented by gene names ranked by expression in 'Source:', after stimulation with the listed molecule.\n\nPerturbation: triptorelin\n\nPerturbation dose: 0.37 uM\n\nPerturbation time: 24 h\n\nCanonical SMILE: CC(C)C[C@H](NC(=O)[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]c2ccccc12)NC(=O)[C@H] ...\n\nCell line: MCF7\n\nSource: GAPDH HSPB1 MIF RPS6 PCNA XBP1 TIMM9 RNPS1 GNAS RPN1 APP S100A13 STUB1 ...\n\nResponse:\n\nGAPDH GNAS RPS6 RNPS1 S100A13 DFFB XBP1 TIMM9 NCOA3 FKBP4 CSRP1 LSM5 ...\n\n#### Perturbation effect prediction - SciPlex3\n\nPrompt: Determine the single cell's expression changes, listed in 'Untreated:', under the outlined conditions after exposure to the specified molecule.\n\nPerturbation: Enzastaurin (LY317615)\n\nPerturbation dose: 1000.0\n\nSMILES: CN1C=C(C2=CC=CC=C21)C3=C(C(=O)NC3=O)C4=CN(C5=CC=CC=C54)C6CCN(CC6) ...\n\nCell line: A549\n\nPathway: TGF-beta/Smad\n\nPathway level 1: PKC signaling\n\nPathway level 2: PKC activitiy\n\nUntreated: PDE4D AKR1B10 AKR1C2 CA12 GAPDH PDE10A ALDH1A1 GLIS3 CNTN1 PDE4B MYO1E ABCC3 PCDH9 SQSTM1 FN1 PTPRM PDE7B TXNRD1 FAM107B EFNA5 PTK2 GABRB3 ...\n\nResponse: PDE4D GAPDH CYP24A1 ALDH1A1 MAP7 ABCC2 MYO1E ALDH3A1 AKR1C2 CAST TXNRD1 AKR1B10 LINC00511 PRKCA NCOA3 AC099520.1 NRG1 PLD5 SMAD3 STAT3 AKR1C3 ...\n\nFigure 15: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale downstream perturbation prediction task on cytokine stimulation PBMC data, L1000, and SciPlex3.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\n#### Spatial neighborhood prediction\n\nPrompt: The following are 6 cell sentences from human liver tissue. Determine if these cells belong to the same spatial neighborhood.\n\nCell 1: HLA-A APOE B2M APOC1 MALAT1 GC ...\n\nCell 2: MALAT1 HLA-A APOE CLU CTSD SOD2 ...\n\nCell 3: FN1 HLA-A SERPINA1 CTSD MALAT1 ENO1 ...\n\nCell 4: VTN APOE HLA-A SERPINA1 MALAT1 APOC1 ...\n\nCell 5: HLA-A SERPINA1 APOE B2M FN1 IL32 PGK1 ...\n\nCell 6: GPX1 HLA-A CALM3 DUSP1 ITGB2 WNT10B ...\n\nResponse: No.\n\n#### Same niche prediction\n\nPrompt: The following are 6 cell sentences from human liver tissue. Determine if these cells belong to the same niche.\n\nCell 1: APOA1 SERPINA1 TTR APOC1 APOE GC MALAT1 ...\n\nCell 2: SERPINA1 APOA1 APOC1 TTR APOE GC FGG ...\n\nCell 3: SERPINA1 APOA1 TTR APOC1 APOE VTN GC MALAT1 ...\n\nCell 4: APOA1 SERPINA1 APOC1 TTR APOE VTN SERPINA3 ...\n\nCell 5: SERPINA1 APOA1 TTR APOC1 APOE GC VTN FGG ...\n\nCell 6: APOA1 SERPINA1 APOC1 TTR APOE VTN CLU ...\n\nResponse: Yes, these cells belong to the Zone 2b niche.\n\n#### Spatial generation\n\nPrompt: The following are 7 cell sentences from the same spatial neighborhood in human liver tissue. Generate\n\na novel cell sentence belonging to the same neighborhood.\n\nCell 1: HLA-A B2M PGK1 FGG SERPINA1 MT2A CTSD ...\n\nCell 2: HLA-A APOE B2M ENO1 SERPINA1 MALAT1 APOC1 ...\n\nCell 3: SPP1 HLA-A SH3BGRL3 CTSD HMGN2 MALAT1 ...\n\nCell 4: PGK1 APOE ENO1 CDKN1A APOC1 HLA-DRB1 ...\n\nCell 5: SPP1 HLA-A B2M PSAP HMGN2 APOE CTSD LYZ ...\n\nCell 6: HLA-A APOE SERPINA1 MALAT1 APOC1 IL32 ENO1 ...\n\nCell 7: SPP1 HLA-A PSAP CD74 VIM CTSD GPX1 HMGN2 ...\n\nCell sentence:\n\nResponse: HLA-A APOE MALAT1 CTSD CLU HSP90AA1 SPP1 APOC1 ...\n\n#### Niche label prediction\n\nPrompt: The following are 7 cell sentences from human liver tissue. Predict the niche of the cells based on the cell sentences.\n\nCell 1: APOA1 CD5L SERPINA1 APOC1 TTR HLA-A APOE ...\n\nCell 2: APOA1 SERPINA1 TTR APOC1 APOE VTN TPT1 ...\n\nCell 3: APOA1 SERPINA1 APOC1 TTR MALAT1 APOE ...\n\nCell 4: SERPINA1 APOA1 APOC1 TTR APOE VTN GC FGG ...\n\nCell 5: APOA1 SERPINA1 TTR APOC1 MALAT1 APOE VTN ...\n\nCell 6: SERPINA1 C1QB MALAT1 MARCO GPX1 RPL21 ...\n\nCell 7: APOA1 SERPINA1 TTR APOC1 APOE MALAT1 VTN ...\n\nThe niche of the cells is\n\nResponse: Zone 2a.\n\nFigure 16: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale downstream spatial prediction tasks.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 17: Additional results for spatial tasks. A, Spatial cell generation results. Bars indicate average gene overlap percentage across 100 generated replicates for 100 samples. ** = P &lt; 0.01; two-tailed T-test). B, Niche label prediction results. Dashed line indicates random accuracy. * = P &lt; 0.05, ** = P &lt; 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.184). C, Same niche prediction results. Dashed line indicates random accuracy. * = P &lt; 0.05, ** = P &lt; 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.51). D, Mean and standard deviation of C2S models trained with three different random seeds. Including CPDB and BioGRID interactions consistently improves performance over the base C2S model.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific bar-plot figure with panels labeled **A**, **B**, **C**, and **D**.\n\nPanel **A**: Bar chart titled **“Neighborhood Generation”**. Y-axis is **“Gene overlap %”**. Compares variants labeled approximately **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**. Bars increase slightly from left to right, with a significance bracket marked `**` over the comparison.\n\nPanel **B**: Bar chart titled **“Niche Label Prediction”**. Y-axis is **“Niche Prediction Accuracy”**. Methods include **scGPT**, **Nicheformer**, **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**, **Linear Model**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**. A dashed horizontal baseline is shown near the lower part of the plot. Multiple significance brackets are shown with `*` and `**`. Highest bars appear for C2S variants and the linear model, with **C2S (w/ BioGrid)** highest.\n\nPanel **C**: Bar chart titled **“Same Niche Prediction”**. Y-axis is **“Same Niche Prediction Accuracy”**. Methods include **GPT-4o**, **Linear Model**, **scGPT**, **C2S**, **Nicheformer**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**. A dashed baseline is shown. C2S variants with biological database inputs perform better, with **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)** highest. Significance brackets marked `*` and `**` are present.\n\nPanel **D**: Three smaller bar charts with error bars:\n- **Neighborhood Prediction**: compares **C2S**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ CPDB)**; database-augmented versions have higher accuracy.\n- **Niche Label Prediction**: compares **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**; BioGrid variant appears highest.\n- **Same Niche Prediction**: compares **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**; BioGrid variant is clearly highest.\n\nBiological/source objects visible in labels: **BioGrid** and **CPDB**, likely biological interaction/pathway database inputs used to augment C2S. No microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecular structures, or model architecture diagrams are visible. The figure presents quantitative model comparison findings for neighborhood generation/prediction and niche prediction tasks.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 18: Extended characterization and validation of virtual screening candidates in tumor fragment and cell line models. A, Experimental validation of top hits in an immune-context-positive tumor fragment model. Surface levels of HLA-A,B,C (Mean Fluorescence Intensity; MFI) were measured following treatment with 10 nM of each drug. IFNγ serves as a positive control. Significance is compared to DMSO vehicle. B, Computational characterization of the immune-context-neutral state. (Top Left) Baseline Type I interferon (IFN) response gene signature scores for the Primary Tumor context compared to three cell lines (WAGA, UTSCC45, MCC2). (Top Right, Bottom) Volcano plots displaying predicted drug effects in these cell line contexts. Silmitasertib (red marker) shows no significant predicted efficacy in these interferon-low settings compared to the primary tumor context. C, Validation of the interferon-conditional effect in MDK-knockout WAGA cells. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C MFI in MDK-knockout WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of low-dose IFNβ (2 U/ml). D, Generalization of the amplification effect to Type II interferon. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C MFI in MDK-knockout WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of low-dose IFNγ (2 U/ml). Error bars represent mean ± s.d. Significance calculated via two-way t-test with Benjamini-Hochberg correction; ∗ P &lt; 0 . 05 , ∗ ∗ P &lt; 0 . 01 , ∗ ∗ ∗ P &lt; 0 . 001 , ∗ ∗ ∗ ∗ P &lt; 0 . 0001 .\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure with panels A-D.\n\nPanel A: Bar plot titled “Immune Context-Positive Tumor Fragment Model.” Y-axis is “HLA-A/B/C MFI.” Conditions on x-axis: DMSO, Silmitasertib, Afatinib, GSK-126, Crizotinib, Trametinib, IFN-γ. Bars show increased HLA-A/B/C MFI for several treatments, with highest values for IFN-γ and strong increases for Crizotinib/Trametinib. Individual replicate points and significance brackets are shown, including ns p=0.07, *, **, ***, and ****.\n\nPanel B: Computational/model-related panel. Left subpanel is a bar chart titled “Baseline IFN Response,” showing “Baseline IFN-Response Gene Signature Score” for Primary Tumor, WAGA, UTSCC45, and MCC2. Right and lower subpanels are volcano/scatter plots for WAGA, UTSCC45, and MCC2. X-axis is “Predicted Antigen Presentation Score,” y-axis is “-log10(p-value).” Points are colored by treatment labels CX-4945, DMSO, and trametinib, with dashed red p=0.05 threshold lines. This appears to show predicted antigen-presentation effects across cell models/treatments.\n\nPanel C: Bar plot titled “Silmitasertib + IFNβ, WAGA MDK KO.” Y-axis is “HLA ABC MFI.” X-axis is “[Silmitasertib] nM” with doses 0, 30, 100, 300, 1000 under DMSO-only and DMSO + 2 U/ml IFNβ conditions. Biological source/object: WAGA MDK knockout cells. IFNβ plus silmitasertib increases HLA ABC MFI relative to DMSO-only, with dose-associated increases and significance brackets.\n\nPanel D: Bar plot titled “Silmitasertib + IFNγ, WAGA MDK KO.” Y-axis is “HLA ABC MFI.” X-axis is “[Silmitasertib] nM” with doses 0, 10, 30, 100, 300 under DMSO-only and DMSO + 2 U/ml IFNγ conditions. Biological source/object: WAGA MDK knockout cells. IFNγ produces a strong increase in HLA ABC MFI, with additional increases at silmitasertib doses and significance annotations.\n\nOverall visible finding: kinase inhibitor treatments, especially silmitasertib in combination with interferon stimulation, are associated with increased HLA class I / HLA ABC surface expression, and model-derived antigen presentation scores are compared across WAGA, UTSCC45, and MCC2 contexts.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 19: A, Diagram of the scFID metric, computed in foundation model latent space, analogous to FID in computer vision. B, UMAP showing the 10 most frequent cell types in the Dominguez immune dataset [20]. C, The scFID is significantly lower within cells of the same cell type than between different cell types (two sample t-test; 10 samples within cell types, 45 samples between cell types; P = 2 . 23 × 10 -4 ). D, Confusion matrix showing the scFID between every pair of cell types. E-G Same as B-D, but for Leiden clusters instead of cell (two sample t-test; 18 samples within clusters, 153 samples between clusters; P = 2 . 60 × 10 -9 .)\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-G about evaluating single-cell foundation model embeddings with scFID.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. Microscopy-like biological image patches or tissue/cell regions are input to a “Foundation Model.” The model produces embedding distributions across embedding dimensions for two groups shown in blue and red. Each distribution is summarized by mean μ and covariance Σ, then compared using Wasserstein distance to compute scFID.\n\nPanel B: UMAP plot titled “Cell Type Clustering - UMAP.” Points are colored by annotated immune cell types. Axes are UMAP 1 and UMAP 2. The legend includes cell-type categories such as naive thymus-derived CD4-positive cells, memory B cells, CD8-positive alpha-beta memory cells, classical monocytes, CD16-positive/CD56-dim natural killer cells, effector memory CD4-positive cells, alveolar macrophages, CD4-positive helper T cells, T follicular helper cells, and effector memory CD8-positive cells.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot titled “Cell Type - scFID Comparison.” It compares scFID for Inter versus Within cell-type groups. Inter values are higher than Within values, with a significance marker shown above the comparison.\n\nPanel D: Heatmap titled “Cell Type - scFID Matrix.” Rows and columns are the same immune cell-type labels. The matrix shows pairwise scFID values, with darker/lighter blue indicating magnitude according to a colorbar.\n\nPanel E: UMAP plot titled “Leiden Clustering (res=0.5) - UMAP.” Points are colored by Leiden clusters labeled Cluster 0 through Cluster 17. Axes are UMAP 1 and UMAP 2.\n\nPanel F: Boxplot titled “Leiden - scFID Comparison.” It compares Inter versus Within scFID for Leiden clusters. Inter-cluster scFID is higher than within-cluster scFID, with a significance marker.\n\nPanel G: Heatmap titled “Leiden - scFID Matrix.” Pairwise scFID values are shown between Leiden clusters 0-17, with a blue color scale and numeric values in cells.\n\nVisible finding: scFID separates between-group comparisons from within-group comparisons for both annotated cell types and Leiden clusters, with inter-group distances generally larger than within-group distances.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 20: The 97 genes in the 'Hallmark Interferon Alpha Response' gene set, used as a proxy for estimating the level of baseline interferon activity for virtual screening.\n\n| Interferon response gene set                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            